window.PA_WEEKLY_CASES = [{"explain": "A supplier in another country was owed EUR 180,000.\nThe payment left the ordering bank on Monday morning.\nBy Friday, the supplier had received nothing.\nThe ordering bank insisted the money had been sent.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ pain.001 received from the corporate customer\n→ pacs.008 sent to the beneficiary bank with the full instruction\n→ pacs.009 cover sent separately through a correspondent bank\n→ The cover payment was held for a funding check at the intermediary bank\n→ The beneficiary bank received the instruction but no funds\n→ The beneficiary bank waited, and credited nothing\n→ pacs.002 showed the instruction as accepted, not settled\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Send the instruction and the cover together, and the credit will follow.\"\n\nSo the operations team watched the instruction status and stopped there. The instruction status said accepted, so everyone assumed the funds had followed. Nobody checked whether the cover had actually arrived, and nobody owned the match between the two legs until the supplier complained.\n\nWhy?\n\nIn the cover method, the instruction and the money travel on different paths. The pacs.008 tells the beneficiary bank what to credit. The pacs.009 moves the funds between banks through correspondent accounts. The beneficiary bank cannot credit the supplier until the funds arrive, however complete the instruction looks. In the serial method, the instruction and the funds pass through each bank together, and each intermediary deducts its charge before passing the payment on.\n\nHere the cover stalled at the intermediary bank, and the two legs drifted apart. The ordering bank could prove it had sent the instruction. The beneficiary bank could prove it had received no funds. Both statements were true, and the supplier still had no money. The missing piece was never a message. It was the match between the instruction and the cover, and that match had no owner, no alert, and no deadline.\n\nAn instruction without its cover is a promise the beneficiary bank cannot keep.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nWhen a payment uses cover, the requirement must cover both legs, not just the message that leaves first. Track the instruction and the cover as one payment, matched on the same reference, and raise an alert when one leg arrives without the other. The customer update should describe funds, not messages, and it should say which leg is still missing.\n\n→ pacs.008, the instruction to credit the beneficiary\n→ pacs.009, the cover that moves the funds between banks\n→ The match, instruction and cover joined on the same reference\n→ pacs.002, the status of each leg, checked separately\n→ The alert, raised when a leg waits longer than the agreed window\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When did the instruction leave?\"\n\nIt is \"What proves the instruction and the funds have both arrived?\"\n\nHave you ever chased a payment where the message arrived but the money did not?", "id": "WC-01", "level": "core", "module": "Core", "seq": 1, "source": "Public post", "takeaway": "An instruction without its cover is a promise the beneficiary bank cannot keep.", "title": "EUR 180,000 sent, the instruction arrived, the money did not", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "pacs.008 arrived, the cover did not, no credit", "parts": [{"label": "pacs.008", "note": "The instruction reached the beneficiary bank in full"}, {"label": "pacs.009", "note": "The cover moved the funds between banks, and stalled"}, {"label": "Intermediary bank", "note": "Held the cover for a funding check"}, {"label": "Beneficiary bank", "note": "Received the instruction but no funds to credit"}, {"label": "pacs.002", "note": "Showed accepted, which is not the same as settled"}, {"label": "The match", "note": "Instruction and cover joined on one reference, unowned"}]}}, "week": 1, "agenda": "Work through this case: EUR 180,000 sent, the instruction arrived, the money did not. A supplier in another country was owed EUR 180,000. The payment left the ordering bank on Monday morning. By Friday, the supplier had received nothing. The ordering bank insisted the money had been sent. One thing to remember: An instruction without its cover is a promise the beneficiary bank cannot keep.", "thumbnail": "assets/thumbs/case-WC-01.svg"}, {"explain": "A customer sent USD 95,000 to a family member abroad.\nThe gpi tracker showed the payment as credited.\nThe family member checked the account twice a day for a week.\nNo money ever arrived.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ pain.001 raised, pacs.008 sent with a tracking reference\n→ The gpi tracker showed ACSP, settlement in progress\n→ The tracker then showed ACSC, settlement completed\n→ The sending bank closed the case as delivered\n→ The receiving bank had credited a suspense account, not the beneficiary account\n→ The beneficiary account number on the instruction did not match the account held\n→ No pacs.004 return was sent, because the receiving bank still held the funds\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"If the tracker says credited, the beneficiary has the money.\"\n\nSo the sending bank told the customer the payment was complete, and the customer told the family member to look again. Both sides trusted the tracker. The tracker was reporting the last status it had received, and nobody asked what account the credit had actually landed in.\n\nWhy?\n\nACSP means the payment is accepted and settlement is in progress. ACSC means the creditor agent has confirmed settlement is complete. The distinction matters, because ACSC confirms the creditor agent's position, not the final step into the beneficiary's account. A credit can be complete at the bank and still sit in a suspense account, a pooled account, or the wrong sub-account while the beneficiary sees nothing.\n\nHere the receiving bank had confirmed ACSC when it applied the funds internally, before the last mile check matched the account number to a live account. The tracker was not lying. It was reporting a bank level event as if it were a customer level event. The last mile, from the bank's books to the beneficiary's available balance, had no status of its own, so its failure stayed invisible until the customer complained loudly enough.\n\nCredited at the bank is not the same as available to the beneficiary.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nTracker statuses need plain language and a clear owner for the last mile. ACSC should never be shown to a customer as received until the beneficiary account itself has been credited. Build a check that reconciles the tracker status against the actual account posting, and treat any gap as an open case, not a closed one.\n\n→ ACSP, settlement in progress, the payment is still moving\n→ ACSC, the creditor agent confirms settlement is complete\n→ The last mile, the credit from the bank's books to the account\n→ The suspense account, where unmatched credits wait\n→ pacs.028, the status request that asks what actually happened\n\nThe real requirement is not \"What does the tracker show?\"\n\nIt is \"What proves the beneficiary's account was credited and the funds are available?\"\n\nHave you ever trusted a tracker status that turned out to mean something different from what the customer heard?", "id": "WC-02", "level": "core", "module": "Core", "seq": 2, "source": "Public post", "takeaway": "Credited at the bank is not the same as available to the beneficiary.", "title": "Tracker said credited, the beneficiary saw nothing for a week", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "pacs.008 sent", "note": "USD 95,000 instruction leaves with a tracking reference."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "ACSP shown", "note": "Tracker shows settlement in progress."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "ACSC shown", "note": "Tracker shows settlement completed."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Account matched?", "note": "Account number does not match a live account."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Suspense credit", "note": "Funds sit in a suspense account, not the beneficiary account."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Case closed", "note": "Sending bank closes the case, customer still has no money."}]}}, "week": 2, "agenda": "Work through this case: Tracker said credited, the beneficiary saw nothing for a week. A customer sent USD 95,000 to a family member abroad. The gpi tracker showed the payment as credited. The family member checked the account twice a day for a week. No money ever arrived. One thing to remember: Credited at the bank is not the same as available to the beneficiary.", "thumbnail": "assets/thumbs/case-WC-02.svg"}, {"explain": "A beneficiary received EUR 42,000 on a Monday.\nBy the following week, the money was spent.\nEleven days after the credit, a pacs.004 return arrived.\nThe bank debited the account, and the balance went negative.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ pacs.008 received and the beneficiary account credited in full\n→ No pacs.002 reject, no warning, no hold placed on the funds\n→ The beneficiary paid suppliers and treated the money as final\n→ The ordering bank discovered the instruction had the wrong beneficiary details\n→ The beneficiary bank investigated and agreed the credit was wrongly applied\n→ pacs.004 returned the funds eleven days after the original credit\n→ The debit left the beneficiary overdrawn and facing supplier claims\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Credit the account when the instruction arrives, and returns will sort themselves out.\"\n\nSo the credit went in cleanly, the return came back slowly, and the customer in the middle carried the whole loss. The bank recovered its funds. The beneficiary, who had done nothing wrong except spend money that appeared to be theirs, was left with the overdraft and the embarrassment of telling suppliers the payment had vanished.\n\nWhy?\n\nA credit is not the same as final settlement. A pacs.004 return can follow days after the original payment when the ordering bank discovers an error, the beneficiary bank finds the credit was misapplied, or a recall through camt.056 is accepted. Until the return window and the investigation window have passed, the funds carry a risk that the customer never sees on the statement.\n\nThe dilemma is who bears that risk. The beneficiary acted reasonably on money that was showing as available. The bank acted correctly in returning funds that were never meant for that account. Both are true, and the loss still lands somewhere. Freezing every incoming credit until it is beyond return would protect the bank and punish every honest beneficiary. Crediting immediately and returning late protects speed and punishes the one customer who spends first.\n\nAvailable on the statement is not the same as beyond return.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nRequirements must state the return window, the hold policy, and who is told what. Large or unusual credits may need a short availability note, and any return after the customer has drawn on the funds needs a human conversation before an automatic debit. The system should flag a late return as an exception, not process it as routine.\n\n→ pacs.008, the original credit instruction\n→ The credit, applied to the beneficiary account in full\n→ camt.056, the recall request from the ordering bank\n→ pacs.004, the return, eleven days later\n→ The debit, recovering funds the customer had already spent\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we credit the account?\"\n\nIt is \"How long can a credit be returned, and who bears the loss if it is?\"\n\nWould you spend money that was showing as available, knowing a return could still arrive days later?", "id": "WC-03", "level": "advanced", "module": "Advanced", "seq": 3, "source": "Public post", "takeaway": "Available on the statement is not the same as beyond return.", "title": "EUR 42,000 returned 11 days later, after it was spent", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Credit spent, return arrived 11 days later", "parts": [{"label": "pacs.008", "note": "The original instruction, credited in full on Monday"}, {"label": "The credit", "note": "Showing as available, spent within the week"}, {"label": "camt.056", "note": "The recall request when the error was found"}, {"label": "pacs.004", "note": "The return, eleven days after the credit"}, {"label": "The debit", "note": "Funds recovered, balance left negative"}, {"label": "Return window", "note": "The risk period the customer never sees"}]}}, "week": 3, "agenda": "Work through this case: EUR 42,000 returned 11 days later, after it was spent. A beneficiary received EUR 42,000 on a Monday. By the following week, the money was spent. Eleven days after the credit, a pacs.004 return arrived. The bank debited the account, and the balance went negative. One thing to remember: Available on the statement is not the same as beyond return.", "thumbnail": "assets/thumbs/case-WC-03.svg"}, {"explain": "A corporate customer paid USD 60,000 to an overseas supplier.\nThe customer chose OUR, so the supplier would receive the full amount.\nThe supplier received USD 59,940.\nThe customer was also charged a fee at the sending bank, and nobody could explain the missing USD 60.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ pain.001 marked OUR, the sender bears all charges\n→ pacs.008 carried the OUR instruction through the chain\n→ The sending bank charged its own fee to the customer\n→ An intermediary bank deducted USD 60, despite the OUR instruction\n→ The beneficiary bank credited the reduced amount\n→ The customer complained, and each bank pointed to the next bank\n→ No bank owned the second charge, so no bank refunded it\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"If the instruction says OUR, every bank in the chain will honour it.\"\n\nSo the customer paid twice for the same promise. Once in the fee at the sending bank, and once in the deduction that was never supposed to happen. The supplier, who had quoted a fixed price, asked the customer to make up the shortfall, and a clean commercial deal turned into a three-way argument about USD 60.\n\nWhy?\n\nOUR means the ordering customer bears all charges, SHA means charges are shared between sender and beneficiary, and BEN means the beneficiary bears them. OUR is an instruction to the chain, not a guarantee. An intermediary bank outside the scheme rules, or one whose systems do not read the charge instruction correctly, can still deduct its fee. The instruction travels in the message, but the deduction happens in the intermediary's own books, where the instruction may never be checked.\n\nThe non-obvious lesson is that charge discipline is a chain problem, not a message problem. The sending bank cannot force an intermediary it has no relationship with to comply. The beneficiary bank cannot restore money it never received. The customer has no contract with the intermediary at all. Until someone in the chain owns the end to end charge outcome, OUR remains a request that usually works, and the failures land on the party with the least power to fix them.\n\nOUR is an instruction to the chain, not a guarantee the chain will obey.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nRequirements must define what happens when a charge instruction is breached. Log the amount sent and the amount credited, reconcile the difference against the charge instruction, and raise a fee claim automatically when an OUR payment arrives short. The customer message should say what was deducted, by which leg, and who is claiming it back.\n\n→ OUR, the sender bears all charges\n→ SHA, charges shared between sender and beneficiary\n→ BEN, the beneficiary bears all charges\n→ The deduction, taken by an intermediary despite the OUR instruction\n→ The fee claim, the route to recovering a wrongful deduction\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Which charge code did the customer choose?\"\n\nIt is \"What proves the charge instruction was honoured end to end, and who refunds the customer when it was not?\"\n\nHave you ever seen a customer charged twice for the same payment, with every bank blaming the next one?", "id": "WC-04", "level": "core", "module": "Core", "seq": 4, "source": "Public post", "takeaway": "OUR is an instruction to the chain, not a guarantee the chain will obey.", "title": "OUR charges, USD 60 deducted anyway, nobody owned it", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "OUR chosen", "note": "Customer chooses OUR, sender bears all charges."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Fee charged", "note": "Sending bank charges its own fee to the customer."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "OUR honoured?", "note": "Intermediary deducts USD 60 despite the instruction."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Short credit", "note": "Beneficiary bank credits USD 59,940, not USD 60,000."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Complaint loops", "note": "Each bank points to the next bank in the chain."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "No refund", "note": "No bank owns the second charge, so no bank refunds it."}]}}, "week": 4, "agenda": "Work through this case: OUR charges, USD 60 deducted anyway, nobody owned it. A corporate customer paid USD 60,000 to an overseas supplier. The customer chose OUR, so the supplier would receive the full amount. The supplier received USD 59,940. The customer was also charged a fee at the sending bank, and nobody could explain the missing USD 60. One thing to remember: OUR is an instruction to the chain, not a guarantee the chain will obey.", "thumbnail": "assets/thumbs/case-WC-04.svg"}, {"explain": "A customer sent EUR 100,000 to be paid in US dollars.\nThe rate on the morning of the instruction was agreed and confirmed.\nThe beneficiary received USD 1,800 less than the confirmed figure.\nThe bank said the rate had moved. The customer said the rate had been agreed.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ pain.001 instructed in euro, with conversion to US dollars\n→ The sending bank quoted a rate and a value date\n→ The payment was converted at the intermediary bank, not the sending bank\n→ The intermediary applied its own rate, on the next business day\n→ The rate had moved overnight, against the customer\n→ The beneficiary bank credited the converted amount, with no query\n→ The customer disputed the shortfall, and the value date with it\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Convert the payment at the rate on the day it is processed.\"\n\nSo the conversion happened at the wrong leg, on the wrong day, at a rate the customer had never seen. The sending bank had quoted one figure. The intermediary had applied another. The customer was left arguing about a rate movement that nobody had warned them about, on a payment they had believed was fixed.\n\nWhy?\n\nIn a cross-border payment, conversion can happen at the ordering bank, at an intermediary bank, or at the beneficiary bank, and each leg uses its own rate and its own value date. The value date is the date the funds are available and the date the rate is struck. When the leg that converts is not the leg that quoted, the customer bears a rate they never agreed, and a value date they never chose.\n\nThe dilemma is that the rate movement is real, and the customer's expectation is also real. Markets move, and no bank can hold a rate forever. But a bank that quotes a rate and a value date, then lets another bank convert a day later, has transferred its own processing delay into the customer's pocket. The question is not whether the rate moved. It is who owned the delay that let the movement bite.\n\nThe leg that converts owns the rate, and the delay that moves it.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nRequirements must name the converting leg, the rate source, the rate validity window, and the value date, before the payment is released. If the converting leg changes, or the value date slips, the customer must be told before settlement, not after. Any dispute needs the rate, the time it was struck, and the leg that struck it, all on one record.\n\n→ The quote, rate and value date agreed with the customer\n→ The converting leg, the bank that actually applies the rate\n→ The value date, the date the funds are available\n→ The rate movement, overnight, against the customer\n→ The dispute, shortfall plus value date, owned by one team\n\nThe real requirement is not \"What rate applied on the processing day?\"\n\nIt is \"Which leg converts, at what rate, on what value date, and who bears the movement if that slips?\"\n\nIf a quoted rate slips by a day and the customer loses money, who should bear the movement?", "id": "WC-05", "level": "advanced", "module": "Advanced", "seq": 5, "source": "Public post", "takeaway": "The leg that converts owns the rate, and the delay that moves it.", "title": "FX converted at the wrong leg, USD 1,800 vanished overnight", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Conversion at the wrong leg, on the wrong day", "parts": [{"label": "The quote", "note": "Rate and value date agreed on the morning"}, {"label": "Sending bank", "note": "Quoted the rate, but did not convert"}, {"label": "Intermediary bank", "note": "Converted a day later at its own rate"}, {"label": "Value date", "note": "The date the funds are available, slipped a day"}, {"label": "Rate movement", "note": "Overnight move, against the customer"}, {"label": "The shortfall", "note": "USD 1,800 less than the confirmed figure"}]}}, "week": 5, "agenda": "Work through this case: FX converted at the wrong leg, USD 1,800 vanished overnight. A customer sent EUR 100,000 to be paid in US dollars. The rate on the morning of the instruction was agreed and confirmed. The beneficiary received USD 1,800 less than the confirmed figure. The bank said the rate had moved. The customer said the rate had been agreed. One thing to remember: The leg that converts owns the rate, and the delay that moves it.", "thumbnail": "assets/thumbs/case-WC-05.svg"}, {"explain": "A payment of EUR 75,000 was sent to pay for urgent medical equipment.\nThe payment never left the bank.\nA sanctions screening match froze it in the queue.\nThe hospital waited, and the equipment supplier waited, while the case sat with compliance.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ pain.001 received for a hospital supplier payment\n→ Sanctions screening flagged a name similarity with a listed entry\n→ The payment was held automatically, with no customer message\n→ Operations asked compliance to release it urgently\n→ Compliance needed evidence that the match was a false positive\n→ Evidence took three days to gather and check\n→ The payment was released, after the delivery slot had been missed\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"If screening flags a payment, holding it is always the safe choice.\"\n\nSo the safe choice for the bank became the harmful choice for the hospital. The hold protected the bank from a sanctions breach. It also delayed equipment that patients were waiting for. Nobody in the chain wanted that outcome, and nobody in the chain had the authority to weigh the two harms against each other in time.\n\nWhy?\n\nSanctions screening matches names, and names collide. A false positive is a match that looks like a listed person or entity but is not. The system must hold the payment while a human checks, because releasing a true match would breach sanctions law. But a hold with no clock, no escalation path, and no customer message turns a control into a blockage, and the cost of the blockage falls on people who have no part in the sanctions risk.\n\nThe dilemma is genuine. Compliance cannot release on a promise, and operations cannot treat every hold as routine. A hospital payment, a food shipment, or a payroll run carries a human cost when it waits. The answer is not to weaken screening. It is to give urgent, low risk holds a faster lane, with same day human review, documented reasoning, and a customer message that says what is happening and when it will be decided.\n\nHolding a payment is also a decision, and it has a cost.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nRequirements must give every screening hold a clock, an owner, and an escalation path. False positive handling needs a documented evidence checklist, a priority lane for time critical payments, and a customer notification at the moment of the hold. The audit trail must show why the payment was held, what was checked, and who decided to release it.\n\n→ The screening flag, a name similarity, not a confirmed match\n→ The hold, automatic, with no customer message\n→ The evidence check, proving the match is a false positive\n→ The escalation lane, faster review for urgent payments\n→ The release, documented, with the reasoning recorded\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Do we hold every flagged payment?\"\n\nIt is \"How quickly can a human confirm a false positive, and who tells the customer what is happening?\"\n\nWhen a control protects the bank but harms the customer, how fast must the review be?", "id": "WC-06", "level": "advanced", "module": "Advanced", "seq": 6, "source": "Public post", "takeaway": "Holding a payment is also a decision, and it has a cost.", "title": "False positive froze a hospital payment for three days", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Payment received", "note": "EUR 75,000 hospital supplier payment enters screening."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Screening match?", "note": "Name similarity flags a possible listed entry."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Automatic hold", "note": "Payment held, no message sent to the customer."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Evidence check", "note": "Three days to prove the match is a false positive."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Release", "note": "Payment released after the delivery slot was missed."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "The cost", "note": "Hospital waits, supplier waits, patients wait."}]}}, "week": 6, "agenda": "Work through this case: False positive froze a hospital payment for three days. A payment of EUR 75,000 was sent to pay for urgent medical equipment. The payment never left the bank. A sanctions screening match froze it in the queue. The hospital waited, and the equipment supplier waited, while the case sat with compliance. One thing to remember: Holding a payment is also a decision, and it has a cost.", "thumbnail": "assets/thumbs/case-WC-06.svg"}, {"explain": "A payment of GBP 250,000 had to settle on Friday.\nThe funds were debited from the customer on Friday morning.\nThe payment never left the bank that day.\nOn Monday, the deal it was funding had already failed.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ pain.001 accepted, customer account debited, value date Friday\n→ The payment needed funding from the nostro account in the settlement currency\n→ The nostro balance was short, after an earlier large payment\n→ Treasury was not alerted until after the cut-off time\n→ The payment queued to the next business day\n→ The customer was told nothing until Monday morning\n→ The receiving party treated the late payment as a failed deal\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Queue any payment that cannot be funded, and release it when funds arrive.\"\n\nSo the system did exactly what it was told, and the customer lost the deal anyway. The queue was silent. The customer believed Friday settlement was happening, because the debit had already appeared. Nobody connected the funding shortfall to the customer's deadline until the deadline had passed.\n\nWhy?\n\nA nostro account is the bank's own account with a correspondent bank, held in a foreign currency, and it is the pot from which cross-border payments are funded. When the balance is short at cut-off, the payment cannot settle, however good the instruction is. Funding is a treasury decision, and treasury can only act if it knows the shortfall in time. A queue without an alert is not liquidity management. It is hope.\n\nThe non-obvious lesson is that the failure was not the shortfall. Shortfalls happen every day, and treasury covers them routinely. The failure was the silence between the queue and the customer. A warning on Friday morning would have let the customer renegotiate, fund differently, or warn the counterparty. The queue protected the bank's books and abandoned the customer's deal, because no requirement said the customer must be told.\n\nA queued payment the customer does not know about is a broken promise, not a delay.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nRequirements must link funding, cut-off, and customer communication. A payment that cannot fund by an agreed time needs a treasury alert, a customer warning, and a decision record, all before cut-off. The value date promise must be conditional on funding, and the customer must see that condition before the payment is accepted.\n\n→ The nostro account, the funding pot in the settlement currency\n→ The shortfall, discovered too late to fix before cut-off\n→ The cut-off time, after which same day settlement is gone\n→ The queue, silent, holding the payment to Monday\n→ The customer warning, the message that never went out\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we queue an unfunded payment?\"\n\nIt is \"How early do we warn the customer that funding may miss the value date?\"\n\nHave you ever seen a payment queue silently while the customer's deadline passed?", "id": "WC-07", "level": "advanced", "module": "Advanced", "seq": 7, "source": "Public post", "takeaway": "A queued payment the customer does not know about is a broken promise, not a delay.", "title": "Nostro shortfall at cut-off, GBP 250,000 queued, deal failed", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Nostro short, payment queued, deal failed", "parts": [{"label": "Nostro account", "note": "The funding pot in the settlement currency"}, {"label": "The shortfall", "note": "Balance short after an earlier large payment"}, {"label": "Cut-off time", "note": "After cut-off, same day settlement is gone"}, {"label": "Treasury", "note": "Alerted too late to fund before cut-off"}, {"label": "The queue", "note": "Silent hold to the next business day"}, {"label": "The customer", "note": "Debited Friday, told nothing until Monday"}]}}, "week": 7, "agenda": "Work through this case: Nostro shortfall at cut-off, GBP 250,000 queued, deal failed. A payment of GBP 250,000 had to settle on Friday. The funds were debited from the customer on Friday morning. The payment never left the bank that day. On Monday, the deal it was funding had already failed. One thing to remember: A queued payment the customer does not know about is a broken promise, not a delay.", "thumbnail": "assets/thumbs/case-WC-07.svg"}, {"explain": "A supplier was owed EUR 30,000.\nThe supplier was paid twice.\nThe customer was debited twice.\nBoth payments looked correct, and both had different tracking references.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ pain.001 sent, pacs.008 dispatched to the beneficiary bank\n→ The response timed out, and no pacs.002 status report arrived\n→ The sending system assumed the payment had failed\n→ A second pacs.008 was sent, with a new tracking reference\n→ The first pacs.008 had in fact been received and processed\n→ The beneficiary bank credited EUR 30,000 twice\n→ Duplicate detection failed, because the two instructions looked like two payments\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"If no status report arrives, the payment did not go through.\"\n\nSo a timeout became a double payment, and the recovery became a negotiation. The supplier had received money it was not owed, and the customer had to ask for it back. The bank could not simply reverse the second credit, because the funds were already in the beneficiary's account and the beneficiary's consent was needed to return them.\n\nWhy?\n\nA timeout means the response was lost, not that the payment failed. The pacs.008 may have been received, processed, and credited, while the pacs.002 confirming it never came back. Resending with a new tracking reference creates a second payment in every system it touches, because the unique end to end transaction reference, the UETR, is the only thing that lets the beneficiary bank recognise a duplicate. The same UETR, reused, would have been caught. A new reference sailed straight through.\n\nThe dilemma sits in the retry design. Retrying quickly protects the customer from a failed payment. Retrying blindly creates a duplicate. The safe path is to ask before resending: send a pacs.028 status request, wait for the pacs.029 response, and only resend when the investigation confirms the first instruction never arrived. Speed without a status check is not resilience. It is a second payment waiting to happen.\n\nA timeout is a missing answer, not a failed payment. Ask before you resend.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nRequirements must treat every retry as a controlled event. Reuse the same UETR for any resend, require a pacs.028 investigation before a second instruction is created, and build duplicate detection on the reference plus amount plus beneficiary, not on any one field alone. Any double credit needs a recall path through camt.056 and a customer conversation, not an automatic reversal.\n\n→ pacs.008, the first instruction, sent and received\n→ The timeout, no pacs.002, no answer\n→ pacs.028, the status request that should have come next\n→ The resend, a new reference, a second payment\n→ camt.056, the recall request to recover the duplicate\n\nThe real requirement is not \"How quickly do we retry after a timeout?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the first instruction never arrived, before we send a second?\"\n\nWould your retry logic ask first, or would it pay twice?", "id": "WC-08", "level": "advanced", "module": "Advanced", "seq": 8, "source": "Public post", "takeaway": "A timeout is a missing answer, not a failed payment. Ask before you resend.", "title": "Timeout, resend, EUR 30,000 paid twice", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "First pacs.008", "note": "EUR 30,000 instruction sent to the beneficiary bank."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Timeout", "note": "No pacs.002 arrives, the response is lost."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Ask first?", "note": "No pacs.028 sent, system assumes failure."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Resend", "note": "Second pacs.008 sent with a new UETR."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Double credit", "note": "Beneficiary bank credits EUR 30,000 twice."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Recall", "note": "camt.056 recall needs the beneficiary's consent."}]}}, "week": 8, "agenda": "Work through this case: Timeout, resend, EUR 30,000 paid twice. A supplier was owed EUR 30,000. The supplier was paid twice. The customer was debited twice. Both payments looked correct, and both had different tracking references. One thing to remember: A timeout is a missing answer, not a failed payment. Ask before you resend.", "thumbnail": "assets/thumbs/case-WC-08.svg"}, {"explain": "A payment of USD 120,000 arrived in full and on time.\nThe beneficiary could not match it to any invoice.\nThe remittance information had been cut in half.\nThree teams spent a week arguing about whose system had lost the data.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ pain.001 carried full remittance information, invoice numbers and dates\n→ The payment was converted to MT103 for a legacy leg of the journey\n→ Field 70, remittance information, allowed only four lines of 35 characters\n→ The remittance text was truncated to fit, and the invoice numbers were lost\n→ The beneficiary bank credited the funds with the shortened text\n→ The beneficiary's reconciliation system could not match the payment\n→ The payment sat unallocated, while the supplier chased for money already received\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Map the remittance information into field 70.\"\n\nSo the money was perfect and the data was broken, and the broken data caused more work than a failed payment would have. The supplier's ledger showed an unpaid invoice. The customer's ledger showed a paid one. The payment itself was never in doubt, but the reconciliation break made both sides act as if it were.\n\nWhy?\n\nMT103 field 70 holds unstructured remittance information in four lines of 35 characters each. That is enough for a short note, and nowhere near enough for the invoice detail a modern enterprise sends. When a payment passes through a legacy MT leg, the structured data in the pacs.008 is squeezed into field 70, and whatever does not fit is simply lost. The pacs.008 carries structured remittance information that survives the journey. The MT103 does not.\n\nThe non-obvious lesson is that data loss is a payment failure, even when the funds arrive. Reconciliation is how a beneficiary knows what a payment is for, and without it the payment creates manual work, supplier disputes, and duplicate chasing. The requirement treated remittance information as a label. The beneficiary treated it as the payment's identity. The beneficiary was right.\n\nA payment without its remittance data arrives as money and lands as a mystery.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nRequirements must protect remittance data across format changes. Map structured remittance information end to end, flag any truncation before the payment is sent, and warn the customer when a legacy leg will shorten the data. Where the full detail cannot travel with the payment, send it through a separate channel and link it on the same reference.\n\n→ The remittance data, invoice numbers and dates, in full\n→ pacs.008, structured remittance information that survives\n→ MT103 field 70, four lines of 35 characters, and no more\n→ The truncation, invoice numbers lost in the conversion\n→ The reconciliation break, funds received, invoice still open\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Where does the remittance text go?\"\n\nIt is \"What proves the beneficiary can match this payment to its invoice when it arrives?\"\n\nHave you ever seen a perfect payment cause a week of reconciliation work because the data did not survive?", "id": "WC-09", "level": "core", "module": "Core", "seq": 9, "source": "Public post", "takeaway": "A payment without its remittance data arrives as money and lands as a mystery.", "title": "Field 70 truncated the invoice data, USD 120,000 unallocated", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Money arrived, remittance data did not", "parts": [{"label": "pain.001", "note": "Full remittance data, invoice numbers and dates"}, {"label": "pacs.008", "note": "Structured remittance information that survives"}, {"label": "MT103 field 70", "note": "Four lines of 35 characters, and no more"}, {"label": "The truncation", "note": "Invoice numbers lost in the conversion"}, {"label": "Beneficiary bank", "note": "Credited the funds with shortened text"}, {"label": "Reconciliation", "note": "System cannot match the payment, invoice stays open"}]}}, "week": 9, "agenda": "Work through this case: Field 70 truncated the invoice data, USD 120,000 unallocated. A payment of USD 120,000 arrived in full and on time. The beneficiary could not match it to any invoice. The remittance information had been cut in half. Three teams spent a week arguing about whose system had lost the data. One thing to remember: A payment without its remittance data arrives as money and lands as a mystery.", "thumbnail": "assets/thumbs/case-WC-09.svg"}, {"explain": "A payment of EUR 55,000 was rejected three times.\nThe reject reason said the account number was invalid.\nThe beneficiary insisted the account existed, and it did.\nBoth sides were certain, and both sides were right.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ pain.001 raised with an IBAN built from a domestic account number\n→ pacs.008 sent to the beneficiary bank\n→ The beneficiary bank validated the IBAN check digits and rejected it\n→ pacs.002 returned the reject, reason: invalid creditor account number\n→ The customer rechecked the account number with the beneficiary, and resent it unchanged\n→ The payment was rejected again, twice, with the same reason\n→ Investigation found the IBAN had been built with the wrong check digits\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"If the account exists, the IBAN built from it must be valid.\"\n\nSo the customer kept resending a number that could never pass, and the bank kept rejecting a customer who was telling the truth. The account existed. The IBAN did not describe it. Three rejections, three identical reasons, and no message that explained the difference between the two.\n\nWhy?\n\nAn IBAN is not just an account number with a country code in front. It is a structured format with a country code, two check digits, and a bank and account identifier in a country specific layout. The check digits are calculated with a mod-97 algorithm, and if they are wrong the IBAN fails validation before any account is even looked up. The beneficiary bank never reached the account, because the IBAN itself was invalid on arrival.\n\nThe dilemma is that the reject reason describes the symptom, not the cause. Account number invalid sounds like the account does not exist. Here it meant the IBAN as constructed was mathematically invalid, while the underlying account was live and waiting. A reject message that cannot distinguish those two cases will send the customer back to the beneficiary to confirm a number that was never the problem, while the real fault sits in the tool that built the IBAN.\n\nValidating the format is not the same as finding the account.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nRequirements must validate the IBAN at the point of entry, with the mod-97 check and the country format check, and show the customer a clear error before the payment is sent. Reject handling must separate format failures from account failures, and the customer message must say which one happened and what to fix. Never let a customer resend an unchanged IBAN three times.\n\n→ The domestic account number, correct, held by the beneficiary\n→ The IBAN builder, converting the number with wrong check digits\n→ The mod-97 check, failed before any account lookup\n→ pacs.002, reject reason: invalid creditor account number\n→ The fix, rebuild the IBAN, validate it, then resend\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Is the account number correct?\"\n\nIt is \"Does the IBAN as sent pass format and check digit validation, before it leaves the bank?\"\n\nHave you ever chased an account that existed, when the real fault was in the number itself?", "id": "WC-10", "level": "core", "module": "Core", "seq": 10, "source": "Public post", "takeaway": "Validating the format is not the same as finding the account.", "title": "Account existed, IBAN rejected three times, check digits wrong", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "IBAN built", "note": "Domestic account number converted into an IBAN."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "pacs.008 sent", "note": "EUR 55,000 instruction sent to the beneficiary bank."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Check digits valid?", "note": "Mod-97 check fails before any account lookup."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "pacs.002 reject", "note": "Reject reason says invalid creditor account number."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Resent unchanged", "note": "Customer resends the same IBAN, rejected twice more."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "IBAN rebuilt", "note": "Correct check digits, validation passes, payment goes through."}]}}, "week": 10, "agenda": "Work through this case: Account existed, IBAN rejected three times, check digits wrong. A payment of EUR 55,000 was rejected three times. The reject reason said the account number was invalid. The beneficiary insisted the account existed, and it did. Both sides were certain, and both sides were right. One thing to remember: Validating the format is not the same as finding the account.", "thumbnail": "assets/thumbs/case-WC-10.svg"}, {"explain": "A payment of USD 200,000 was ready to go to a long standing trading partner.\nA routine review found the partner was 40 percent owned by a listed person.\nThe payment was not clearly blocked, and not clearly safe.\nThree options sat on the table: block, release, or escalate, and each one carried a risk.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ pain.001 received for a regular trading payment\n→ Screening found no direct match with any sanctions list\n→ An ownership review found a listed person held 40 percent of the beneficiary\n→ A second review found the listed person also controlled the board\n→ Compliance, operations, and the relationship team disagreed on the next step\n→ The payment was escalated, and the customer was given no date for a decision\n→ The trading partner warned that a missed payment would break the supply contract\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"If the name is not on the list, the payment is safe to release.\"\n\nSo the payment sat in the hardest place a payment can sit: not blocked by a rule, and not cleared by one either. Blocking it would breach a commercial contract and damage a relationship built over years. Releasing it could breach sanctions law if control, not just ownership, made the entity blocked. Escalating it bought time, and cost the customer a deadline.\n\nWhy?\n\nSanctions rules reach beyond the names on a list. Under the ownership rule, an entity owned 50 percent or more by a listed person is treated as blocked, and ownership by several listed persons is added together. Below 50 percent, the question turns to control: board seats, veto rights, and the power to direct the entity's actions. A 40 percent holding with board control can be riskier than a 60 percent holding with none, and no screening system can answer that question on its own.\n\nThis is the dilemma that no requirement can fully remove. The law gives a threshold, and judgement must cover everything around it. The right response is a documented decision: who reviewed the ownership and control evidence, what advice was taken, what the decision was, and why. An undocumented release is a risk. An undocumented block is a different risk. The audit trail is what turns a judgement call into a defensible one.\n\nNot on the list is not the same as safe. Ownership and control decide.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nRequirements must define the escalation path for sanctions adjacent cases, with the evidence pack, the decision maker, and the deadline. Screening must cover ownership and control, not just name matching. Every decision to block or release needs a written rationale, a second reviewer, and a customer message that is honest about the wait without disclosing the sanctions reasoning.\n\n→ The name check, no direct match with any list\n→ The ownership review, 40 percent held by a listed person\n→ The control test, board seats and the power to direct\n→ The escalation, documented decision, named decision maker\n→ The customer message, honest about the wait, silent on the reasoning\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Is the name on the list?\"\n\nIt is \"Who decides when ownership and control make a payment neither clearly blocked nor clearly safe?\"\n\nIf a payment is neither clearly blocked nor clearly safe, who in your organisation decides, and what evidence do they need?", "id": "WC-11", "level": "advanced", "module": "Advanced", "seq": 11, "source": "Public post", "takeaway": "Not on the list is not the same as safe. Ownership and control decide.", "title": "40 percent owned by a listed person, block or release?", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Neither blocked nor safe, a judgement call", "parts": [{"label": "Name check", "note": "No direct match with any sanctions list"}, {"label": "Ownership", "note": "40 percent held by a listed person"}, {"label": "Control test", "note": "Board seats and the power to direct"}, {"label": "Block", "note": "Breaches the contract, protects the bank"}, {"label": "Release", "note": "Honours the contract, risks a breach"}, {"label": "Escalate", "note": "Documented decision, named decision maker"}]}}, "week": 11, "agenda": "Work through this case: 40 percent owned by a listed person, block or release?. A payment of USD 200,000 was ready to go to a long standing trading partner. A routine review found the partner was 40 percent owned by a listed person. The payment was not clearly blocked, and not clearly safe. Three options sat on the table: block, release, or escalate, and each one carried a risk. One thing to remember: Not on the list is not the same as safe. Ownership and control decide.", "thumbnail": "assets/thumbs/case-WC-11.svg"}, {"explain": "A customer instructed a payment of EUR 500,000 late in the afternoon.\nThe payment was value dated the next day.\nThe customer lost a full day of interest on half a million euro.\nThe bank said the cut-off had passed. The customer said the instruction was sent in time.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ pain.001 submitted at 16:45 in the customer's time zone\n→ The payment was processed in a time zone two hours ahead\n→ The cut-off for same day value had passed in the processing time zone\n→ The payment was value dated the next business day\n→ Interest was calculated from the value date, not the instruction date\n→ The customer disputed the lost day, citing the instruction time\n→ The bank cited the cut-off schedule, which the customer had never seen\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Apply the cut-off time of the processing centre.\"\n\nSo both clocks were right, and the customer still lost a day. The instruction was in time where the customer sat. It was late where the payment was processed. The cut-off schedule existed, but it lived in an operations manual the customer had never been shown, and the channel gave no warning that the value date had slipped.\n\nWhy?\n\nA cut-off time is the last moment a payment can be accepted for a given value date, and it is set in the time zone of the processing centre or the settlement system, not the customer's. The value date is the date from which interest is calculated and the funds are available. When the two time zones differ, a payment that feels early to the customer can be late to the system, and the value date slips by a day without anyone telling the customer.\n\nThe fault question is the interesting one. The bank followed its schedule. The customer followed the clock on the wall. The real failure was the missing warning: the channel accepted the instruction, showed no value date, and let the customer discover the slip from the interest calculation days later. A cut-off the customer cannot see is not a rule. It is a surprise, and surprises on half a million euro destroy trust faster than any fee.\n\nA cut-off the customer cannot see is a surprise, not a rule.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nRequirements must show the value date and the cut-off in the customer's own time zone, before the payment is confirmed. Any instruction received after cut-off needs a warning and an explicit acceptance of the next value date. The interest impact of a slipped value date should be visible on large payments, not discovered after the event.\n\n→ The instruction time, 16:45 in the customer's time zone\n→ The processing time zone, two hours ahead, cut-off passed\n→ The value date, slipped to the next business day\n→ The interest, calculated from the value date, a day lost\n→ The warning, the message the channel never showed\n\nThe real requirement is not \"What is the cut-off time?\"\n\nIt is \"Does the customer see the value date and the cut-off, in their own time zone, before confirming?\"\n\nHave you ever lost a day of value because two clocks disagreed, and nobody warned you?", "id": "WC-12", "level": "core", "module": "Core", "seq": 12, "source": "Public post", "takeaway": "A cut-off the customer cannot see is a surprise, not a rule.", "title": "Cut-off passed in another time zone, a day of interest lost", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Instruction sent", "note": "pain.001 submitted at 16:45, customer time zone."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Cut-off passed?", "note": "Processing centre is two hours ahead, cut-off passed."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Value date slips", "note": "Payment value dated the next business day."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Interest lost", "note": "A full day of interest lost on EUR 500,000."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Dispute", "note": "Customer cites instruction time, bank cites schedule."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "No warning", "note": "Channel never showed the value date or the cut-off."}]}}, "week": 12, "agenda": "Work through this case: Cut-off passed in another time zone, a day of interest lost. A customer instructed a payment of EUR 500,000 late in the afternoon. The payment was value dated the next day. The customer lost a full day of interest on half a million euro. The bank said the cut-off had passed. The customer said the instruction was sent in time. One thing to remember: A cut-off the customer cannot see is a surprise, not a rule.", "thumbnail": "assets/thumbs/case-WC-12.svg"}, {"explain": "A correspondent bank withdrew from a payment route on a Tuesday.\nForty payments were already in transit on that route.\nSome were held, some were returned, and some simply vanished from the tracker.\nCustomers with deadlines that week had no idea their route no longer existed.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ The correspondent bank gave notice it was exiting the route\n→ The notice reached the operations team after the exit had taken effect\n→ Payments in transit were held at the exiting bank, awaiting instructions\n→ Some payments were returned by pacs.004, with no rerouting offer\n→ The remaining payments were rerouted through a new correspondent bank\n→ Rerouting needed new funding, new screening, and new value dates\n→ Customers learned about the change from missed deliveries, not from the bank\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"If a route closes, the payments on it will find their own way through.\"\n\nSo the exit was orderly on paper and chaotic in practice. The bank that withdrew had followed its own process. The bank that sent the payments had no plan for forty in flight instructions. Each payment was handled as a separate exception, by a different person, on a different day, and the customers were the last to know.\n\nWhy?\n\nDe-risking is when a correspondent bank withdraws from a route, a currency, or a region because the compliance cost or the risk outweighs the return. Payments already in transit do not automatically reroute. The instruction, the cover, and the funding are all tied to the exiting bank, and each one must be unwound and rebuilt on a new route. The pacs.008 may need reissuing, the pacs.009 cover needs new funding, and the payment needs screening again under the new correspondent's rules.\n\nThe dilemma is speed against control. Rerouting fast protects customer deadlines, but skipping screening or funding checks on the new route creates fresh risk. Rerouting carefully protects the bank, but every day of delay breaks another customer promise. The answer is a plan made before the exit: alternate routes agreed in advance, funding lines in place, and a customer message ready to send the moment an exit is announced. A route exit is foreseeable. Treating it as a surprise is a choice.\n\nA route exit is foreseeable. A rerouting plan made after it happens is already late.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nRequirements must include a route exit playbook. Map every route to an alternate correspondent, keep the funding and screening requirements for the alternate current, and define the in flight inventory report that lists every payment on a route within an hour of an exit notice. The customer message must go out before the first missed delivery, not after it.\n\n→ The exit notice, received after the route had closed\n→ The in flight inventory, forty payments, no single list\n→ The holds and returns, pacs.004, no rerouting offer\n→ The alternate route, new correspondent, new funding, new screening\n→ The customer message, sent after the missed deliveries\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Which correspondent handles this route?\"\n\nIt is \"What happens to payments already in transit the hour a route closes, and who tells the customer?\"\n\nIf your main route closed tomorrow, how quickly could you list every payment on it and reroute them?", "id": "WC-13", "level": "advanced", "module": "Advanced", "seq": 13, "source": "Public post", "takeaway": "A route exit is foreseeable. A rerouting plan made after it happens is already late.", "title": "Route closed mid-flight, 40 payments stranded, no plan", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Route exited, 40 payments in transit, no plan", "parts": [{"label": "Exit notice", "note": "Received after the route had already closed"}, {"label": "In flight payments", "note": "Forty payments, no single inventory list"}, {"label": "Holds", "note": "Payments held at the exiting bank"}, {"label": "pacs.004 returns", "note": "Some returned, with no rerouting offer"}, {"label": "Alternate route", "note": "New correspondent, funding, screening, value dates"}, {"label": "Customers", "note": "Learned from missed deliveries, not from the bank"}]}}, "week": 13, "agenda": "Work through this case: Route closed mid-flight, 40 payments stranded, no plan. A correspondent bank withdrew from a payment route on a Tuesday. Forty payments were already in transit on that route. Some were held, some were returned, and some simply vanished from the tracker. Customers with deadlines that week had no idea their route no longer existed. One thing to remember: A route exit is foreseeable. A rerouting plan made after it happens is already late.", "thumbnail": "assets/thumbs/case-WC-13.svg"}, {"explain": "A busy settlement morning, and the RTGS queue stopped moving.\nThree banks each had a large payment ready to send.\nEach bank was waiting for money to arrive before it could pay.\nNobody had failed. The queue had tied itself in a circle.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ The first bank queued a payment of Rs 180 crore, expecting funds from the second bank\n→ The second bank queued a payment of Rs 165 crore, expecting funds from the third bank\n→ The third bank queued a payment of Rs 135 crore, expecting funds from the first bank\n→ Each settlement account held enough for small payments, but not for the large head payment\n→ For 47 minutes money sat in the system, yet none of the three payments settled\n→ A gridlock resolution run offset the three payments, and all three settled together\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"If each bank pays when its incoming funds arrive, the queue will clear itself.\"\n\nIt did not, because in a circle there is no first payment. Each bank behaved sensibly alone, and the three decisions added up to a standstill. No team could see the full circle.\n\nWhy?\n\nRTGS settles each payment individually, in full, in central bank money, only when the paying bank holds enough at that moment. There is no netting inside the queue, and no payment settles on a promise that money is coming. When the head payment cannot settle, those behind it wait too, even small ones that could settle. That head of line blocking turns one large unfunded payment into a blockage for everything behind it.\n\nAggregate liquidity and usable liquidity are different things. Across the three banks there was enough money, because each payment would have funded the next. But RTGS does not settle a circle at once unless a gridlock resolution or offsetting run happens. Until then each bank looked short, and the waiting itself caused the shortage. Intraday liquidity against collateral would have broken the circle in minutes, but no bank wanted to borrow first for a problem it blamed on another bank.\n\nIn RTGS, liquidity is about sequence and timing, not just the total balance.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nA queue is a business state, not a technical holding pen, so it needs visibility, rules and ownership. A BA should ask how the queue is shown, who can reorder or split a payment, what triggers intraday liquidity, and how long a large payment may sit at the head before someone intervenes. The signs are a large payment stuck at the head, small payments ageing behind it, and accounts that look short while expected funds come from banks that are also waiting.\n\nDesign for the circle, not just the single payment:\n\n→ Queue visibility, showing what waits, what it waits for, and for how long\n→ Head of line controls, including reordering, splitting and bypass rules agreed in advance\n→ Gridlock indicators, so operations can spot a circle forming before the queue stalls\n→ Customer communication, because a queued payment is not yet paid\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we send the payment?\"\n\nIt is \"What breaks the circle when every bank is waiting for another bank to move first?\"\n\nHave you ever watched a queue stall even though the money to clear it was already in the system?", "id": "WC-14", "level": "advanced", "module": "Advanced", "seq": 14, "source": "Public post", "takeaway": "In RTGS, liquidity is about sequence and timing, not just the total balance.", "title": "Rs 480 crore stood still while three banks waited for each other", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Three banks, one circle, nothing settles", "parts": [{"label": "First bank queue", "note": "Rs 180 crore waiting for funds from the second bank"}, {"label": "Second bank queue", "note": "Rs 165 crore waiting for funds from the third bank"}, {"label": "Third bank queue", "note": "Rs 135 crore waiting for funds from the first bank"}, {"label": "Settlement accounts", "note": "Enough for small payments, not for the head of queue"}, {"label": "Queue order", "note": "Small payments wait behind the large payment ahead"}, {"label": "Offsetting run", "note": "Gridlock resolution settles the circle together"}]}}, "week": 14, "agenda": "Work through this case: Rs 480 crore stood still while three banks waited for each other. A busy settlement morning, and the RTGS queue stopped moving. Three banks each had a large payment ready to send. Each bank was waiting for money to arrive before it could pay. Nobody had failed. The queue had tied itself in a circle. One thing to remember: In RTGS, liquidity is about sequence and timing, not just the total balance.", "thumbnail": "assets/thumbs/case-WC-14.svg"}, {"explain": "The salary file was ready. The approvals were done.\nThe upload finished forty seconds after the cut-off.\nThat was enough to move 4,200 salary credits to the next working day.\nThe rail was still running. The product window was not.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ The corporate team finished maker checker approval late in the afternoon\n→ The bulk salary file for 4,200 staff was uploaded forty seconds after the bank bulk processing cut-off\n→ The bank system accepted the file but assigned it the next value date\n→ NEFT batches kept running every half hour, but the file was held outside the batch queue\n→ The next value date fell after a bank holiday, so the delay became a full day, not thirty minutes\n→ The corporate team learned about the delay from staff calls, not from the bank\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Files received after the cut-off take the next value date.\"\n\nThe rule worked exactly as written, and treated forty seconds the same as forty hours. Nobody warned the uploader, nobody confirmed the value date, and nobody said a holiday would stretch the delay into a full day. The file was not lost. It was held by a rule nobody had explained as a customer outcome.\n\nWhy?\n\nNEFT settles in half hourly batches and runs around the clock, so the rail itself did not close. What closed was the bank bulk salary processing window, a product rule layered on top of the rail. The bulk product processes one salary run per value date, checks the file, funds the run and releases the credits as a batch. Once the value date rolls, the whole run rolls with it, even though the next NEFT batch is only thirty minutes away. That distinction, between the rail being open and the product window being open, is invisible to the customer until it costs a day.\n\nThe second problem is the clock. Was the file received when the upload started, finished, or was timestamped? Here the receipt timestamp decided the value date. Add a holiday calendar, and a small miss becomes a long wait, because the next value date is the next working day, not the next batch. A cut-off is a promise about when people get paid, and it must be shown that way before submission.\n\nA cut-off is a customer promise in disguise, so it must be visible before it bites.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nCut-off rules must be designed as customer facing behaviour, not buried in a manual. A BA should ask which timestamp counts, how the value date is derived, and how holidays change the outcome. A late file must never be a silent surprise: warn before the cut-off, confirm the value date on receipt, and offer an exception path.\n\nMake the cut-off impossible to miss:\n\n→ Countdown and warning, shown during upload, not discovered afterwards\n→ Receipt timestamp rule, agreed and stated, so start time and finish time are never argued about\n→ Value date confirmation, showing the actual credit date including holidays\n→ Proactive notification, so the corporate team hears it from the bank, not from its staff\n\nThe real requirement is not \"What time does the window close?\"\n\nIt is \"How does the uploader know, before submitting, exactly when the money will arrive?\"\n\nHave you ever seen a small delay at a cut-off turn into a full day of waiting for the customer?", "id": "WC-15", "level": "core", "module": "Core", "seq": 15, "source": "Public post", "takeaway": "A cut-off is a customer promise in disguise, so it must be visible before it bites.", "title": "Forty seconds late, and 4,200 salaries waited a full day", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "File prepared", "note": "Salary file for 4,200 staff approved late in the day."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Before cut-off?", "note": "Upload finishes forty seconds after the bulk cut-off."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Next value date", "note": "File accepted but assigned the next value date."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Batches run on", "note": "NEFT batches continue, but the file sits outside them."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Holiday intervenes", "note": "Next value date falls after a bank holiday."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Salaries late", "note": "Credits arrive a full day after staff expected them."}]}}, "week": 15, "agenda": "Work through this case: Forty seconds late, and 4,200 salaries waited a full day. The salary file was ready. The approvals were done. The upload finished forty seconds after the cut-off. That was enough to move 4,200 salary credits to the next working day. The rail was still running. The product window was not. One thing to remember: A cut-off is a customer promise in disguise, so it must be visible before it bites.", "thumbnail": "assets/thumbs/case-WC-15.svg"}, {"explain": "The corporate customer had been promised same-day credit.\nThe settlement account did not have the funds to keep that promise.\nTreasury saw a liquidity problem. Operations saw a queue. The customer saw a promise.\nOne of the three views had to give way before the end of the day.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ Outgoing payments totalling Rs 340 crore were queued for settlement that afternoon\n→ Incoming funds expected before the cut-off arrived late, leaving a shortfall of Rs 62 crore\n→ The relationship team had already told the customer the credit would land the same day\n→ Treasury could borrow intraday liquidity against collateral, at a cost and with approval\n→ Letting the settlement account close overdrawn would draw a central bank penalty and regulatory attention\n→ The bank borrowed intraday, settled the queue, and repaid before close, but the promise had been made blind\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The funds will arrive in time, so the promise is safe to make.\"\n\nThe promise was made on an expectation, not a balance. No step in the journey required checking the settlement account, forecast or queue. When funds slipped, the bank faced a choice it should never have faced: break the promise, pay for emergency liquidity, or breach settlement rules. Borrowing solved the day but hid the real failure, a promise made without evidence.\n\nWhy?\n\nA settlement account at the central bank is where RTGS payments actually settle, in central bank money. The account must hold enough at the moment each payment settles, and an overdraft at the close of the day is not a normal shortfall, it is a breach that can draw a penalty, draw supervisory attention and damage the standing of the bank in the payment system. Intraday liquidity exists for this situation, funding against collateral repaid before close, but it needs collateral, approval and early warning. None of that helps once the promise has gone out.\n\nThe lesson is about sequencing. Liquidity management is a forecast, a queue and priorities, changing through the day. A same-day promise is a claim on that forecast. Made before the forecast is checked, it commits funds the bank may not hold. Made after, it becomes planned liquidity, with borrowing arranged or the payment prioritised. The difference is the order of the two decisions.\n\nNever promise a settlement time that the settlement account has not confirmed.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nSettlement promises need a funding check built into the journey, not left to memory. A BA should ask what the relationship team sees before promising a time, what the forecast shows, and who owns prioritisation when funds are short. Borrowing, reordering and customer communication are all easier planned early in the day than improvised in the last hour.\n\nPut the funding check before the promise:\n\n→ Liquidity view, showing forecast inflows, outflows and the expected close position\n→ Promise checkpoint, so no same-day commitment is made without a balance or forecast check\n→ Priority rules, agreed in advance, for which payments settle first when funds are short\n→ Intraday borrowing path, with collateral, approval and timing worked out before it is needed\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Can we promise same-day credit?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence must exist before anyone promises a settlement time?\"\n\nHave you ever seen a customer promise made before anyone checked whether the funds were there?", "id": "WC-16", "level": "advanced", "module": "Advanced", "seq": 16, "source": "Public post", "takeaway": "Never promise a settlement time that the settlement account has not confirmed.", "title": "Rs 62 crore short, and a same-day promise already made", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "A promise made without a liquidity check", "parts": [{"label": "Settlement account", "note": "Rs 62 crore short when the queue needed to settle"}, {"label": "Outgoing queue", "note": "Rs 340 crore waiting, largest payment is the promised one"}, {"label": "Intraday liquidity", "note": "Borrowing against collateral, repaid before close"}, {"label": "Central bank penalty", "note": "Cost of closing the day overdrawn, plus scrutiny"}, {"label": "Customer promise", "note": "Same-day credit promised before funds were checked"}, {"label": "Treasury control", "note": "Forecast, priorities and escalation before close"}]}}, "week": 16, "agenda": "Work through this case: Rs 62 crore short, and a same-day promise already made. The corporate customer had been promised same-day credit. The settlement account did not have the funds to keep that promise. Treasury saw a liquidity problem. Operations saw a queue. The customer saw a promise. One of the three views had to give way before the end of the day. One thing to remember: Never promise a settlement time that the settlement account has not confirmed.", "thumbnail": "assets/thumbs/case-WC-16.svg"}, {"explain": "The customer paid a supplier from a mobile app.\nThe money left the account within seconds.\nThe supplier said no payment had arrived.\nFor three days the only status anyone could show was processing.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ The payer app sent the UPI instruction to the payer payment service provider\n→ The switch routed the instruction and recorded a successful switch response\n→ The issuer bank debited the payer account for Rs 86,000\n→ The credit leg to the beneficiary bank timed out, and no final response came back\n→ With no success and no failure, the transaction sat in processing while support teams checked different systems\n→ Reconciliation on the third day confirmed the credit leg had never completed, and the debit was reversed\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Show processing until a final status arrives.\"\n\nThat sounds careful for the first few minutes. After three days it is abandonment. The customer could not pay again without risking a double payment, the supplier could not release the goods, and every support call ended with advice to wait. No team owned the ageing of a transaction with no final status.\n\nWhy?\n\nA UPI payment moves through several parties: the payer app, the payer payment service provider, the switch, the issuer bank that holds the payer account, and the beneficiary bank that holds the payee account. The switch response proves the instruction was routed, not that the beneficiary was credited. The debit proves the issuer acted, not that the credit succeeded. When the credit leg times out, the payment is in an in between state, and the only way to resolve it is a status check followed by reconciliation against settlement records. Until that happens, refunding too early risks a double credit if the late credit then lands, and waiting too long leaves the customer without money and without an answer.\n\nInstant payments still need a slow, careful back office. Timeouts and missing confirmations are normal at scale, and the design must assume them: a status model with ageing rules, an owner for every pending transaction, a deadline for reconciliation to decide, and wording that says what happens next. A payment processing for three days has failed silently, and the silence is the defect.\n\nProcessing is not a status, it is an unresolved payment that needs an owner and a deadline.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nPending states deserve the same design attention as success and failure, because they are where trust is lost. A BA should ask how long a transaction may stay pending, who owns it at each age, and what the customer is told at each stage. A retry must never be offered until the status check confirms the first attempt failed, or the fix becomes a duplicate payment.\n\nDesign the pending state, do not just display it:\n\n→ Status model, with pending, success and failure defined, and ageing rules for pending\n→ Status check discipline, so no retry and no refund happens before the facts are known\n→ Reconciliation ownership, with a deadline for deciding every unresolved transaction\n→ Customer wording, stating what happened, what happens next, and by when\n\nThe real requirement is not \"What do we show while we wait?\"\n\nIt is \"Who owns this payment, and by when must it be resolved?\"\n\nHave you ever watched a payment sit in processing while every party insisted its own part had worked?", "id": "WC-17", "level": "core", "module": "Core", "seq": 17, "source": "Public post", "takeaway": "Processing is not a status, it is an unresolved payment that needs an owner and a deadline.", "title": "Rs 86,000 debited, and the status stayed on processing for three days", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Payer taps pay", "note": "UPI instruction sent for Rs 86,000 to the supplier."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Switch routes", "note": "Switch records success, meaning routing worked."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Issuer debits", "note": "Payer account debited by the issuer bank."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Credit confirmed?", "note": "Credit leg times out, no final response arrives."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Processing", "note": "No success, no failure, for three days."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Reconciliation decides", "note": "Credit never completed, debit is reversed."}]}}, "week": 17, "agenda": "Work through this case: Rs 86,000 debited, and the status stayed on processing for three days. The customer paid a supplier from a mobile app. The money left the account within seconds. The supplier said no payment had arrived. For three days the only status anyone could show was processing. One thing to remember: Processing is not a status, it is an unresolved payment that needs an owner and a deadline.", "thumbnail": "assets/thumbs/case-WC-17.svg"}, {"explain": "The first tap seemed to fail, so the customer tapped again.\nBoth payments settled.\nThe merchant was paid twice for one purchase.\nGetting one payment back took longer than making both of them.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ The customer tapped pay for Rs 12,600 and the app showed a timeout with no clear result\n→ The customer tapped pay again, and the second payment completed at once\n→ The first payment had in fact succeeded, and its confirmation had arrived too late to display\n→ Both debits settled, and both credits landed in the merchant account\n→ The merchant said refunds were a bank matter, the issuer bank said the merchant held the money, and the payment app said both transactions were successful\n→ The duplicate was refunded after nine days, once the beneficiary bank confirmed two credits for one purchase\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"If the customer taps again, the system will recognise the duplicate.\"\n\nIt did not, because the two taps created two transactions with two identifiers. Downstream they looked like two legitimate payments, minutes apart. Nothing asked whether the second tap was a retry, because it was treated as a fresh instruction. The duplicate was visible only to a human comparing the two side by side.\n\nWhy?\n\nA timeout is not a failure. It means the response did not arrive in time, and the payment may have succeeded, failed, or still be in flight. The safe response to a timeout is a status check on the original transaction, using its original identifier, before any new instruction is created. A fresh instruction creates a second payment that settles independently. Once both debits settle and both credits land, the money returns only through refund or dispute, and each party can reasonably say the problem belongs to someone else. The merchant holds the funds, the beneficiary bank holds the account, the issuer bank holds the customer relationship, and the payment app holds the journey.\n\nThe deeper failure is ownership. Duplicate detection should have caught two payments with the same payer, payee, amount and short time gap, and flagged the second before settlement. Failing that, one party should have owned the refund from the moment it was reported. Instead the customer became the coordinator, carrying the story from party to party.\n\nA retry without a status check is a second payment, not a second attempt.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nRetry behaviour is a payments design decision, not a user interface detail. A BA should ask what happens on timeout, whether the retry reuses the original identifier, what duplicate detection runs before settlement, and who owns a duplicate refund end to end. A vague error message plus an active pay button is an invitation to pay twice.\n\nMake a retry safe, or do not offer one:\n\n→ Status check first, on the original identifier, before any new instruction is created\n→ Idempotency, so a repeated submission with the same identifier cannot create a second payment\n→ Duplicate detection, flagging same payer, payee, amount and short time gap before settlement\n→ Timeout wording, telling the customer to wait, not to tap again, with a clear time frame\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Can the customer try again?\"\n\nIt is \"How do we guarantee that trying again can never create a second payment?\"\n\nHave you ever paid twice because the first attempt looked like it had failed?", "id": "WC-18", "level": "core", "module": "Core", "seq": 18, "source": "Public post", "takeaway": "A retry without a status check is a second payment, not a second attempt.", "title": "Two taps, two debits, and every party pointed at another party", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Two debits settled, nobody owns the refund", "parts": [{"label": "First tap", "note": "Timed out on screen, but succeeded in the background"}, {"label": "Retry tap", "note": "New identifier, so a second payment, not a retry"}, {"label": "Issuer bank", "note": "Debited twice, says the merchant holds the money"}, {"label": "Beneficiary bank", "note": "Credited twice, confirms the duplicate after nine days"}, {"label": "Merchant", "note": "Paid twice, says refunds are a bank matter"}, {"label": "Dispute owner", "note": "Missing, so the customer coordinates the claim"}]}}, "week": 18, "agenda": "Work through this case: Two taps, two debits, and every party pointed at another party. The first tap seemed to fail, so the customer tapped again. Both payments settled. The merchant was paid twice for one purchase. Getting one payment back took longer than making both of them. One thing to remember: A retry without a status check is a second payment, not a second attempt.", "thumbnail": "assets/thumbs/case-WC-18.svg"}, {"explain": "A supplier cheque for Rs 3,80,000 was deposited on a Monday.\nIt came back unpaid two days later.\nThe return memo said insufficient funds.\nThe account had held more than enough funds all week.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ The cheque was scanned at the branch and the image was sent through cheque truncation clearing\n→ A fold across the cheque made part of the image unclear, including the signature area\n→ The paying bank could not verify the instrument from the image it received\n→ The return was recorded with the code for insufficient funds instead of the code for an unclear image\n→ Return charges were applied, the supplier was told the cheque had bounced, and the customer record showed a dishonour\n→ The return was corrected, the charges were reversed, and the cheque was presented again, but the supplier relationship had already been damaged\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Return the cheque with the reason the operator selects.\"\n\nThat requirement treats the code as a free choice, and it is not. The code is the formal reason the cheque was not paid, and it travels to the customer, the presenting bank and the customer record. An operator under pressure picked the most familiar code, and the system accepted it without checking the balance, the image, or the verification failure. One selection rewrote a technical problem as a customer failure.\n\nWhy?\n\nCheque truncation means the physical cheque does not travel. The image and the data captured from it are the clearing items, and the paying bank decides on the image it receives. That makes image quality a settlement control, not a scanning nicety. Image quality validation at capture should reject a poor image before it enters clearing. When a poor image does get through, the return must say so, with the technical return code that invites re-presentation of a clear image, not a financial return code that implies the customer could not pay.\n\nThe distinction matters far beyond the branch. A dishonour for insufficient funds carries charges, affects how the payee views the payer, and can carry legal consequences for the drawer. A technical return carries none of that: the instrument could not be verified, try again with a clear image. The wrong code creates a false record, charges the customer for a process failure, and damages a relationship the correction cannot fully repair.\n\nA return reason code is a fact about the customer, not a back office label.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nReturn codes need validation, not just a dropdown. A BA should ask which codes require evidence, what the system checks before accepting each code, and what happens to charges for technical returns. Image quality checks belong at capture, where the cheque can be rescanned. Every return needs a correction path that fixes the record, reverses charges and tells the payee the true reason.\n\nTreat the code as a customer facing fact:\n\n→ Capture quality checks, rejecting unclear images while the cheque can still be rescanned\n→ Code validation, so a funds code requires a funds check, and an image code requires an image finding\n→ No charges on technical returns, because the customer did not cause the failure\n→ Correction process, fixing the record, reversing charges and notifying the payee\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Which code did the operator pick?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves this return reason, and what does it do to the customer?\"\n\nHave you ever seen a customer blamed for a failure that belonged to the process?", "id": "WC-19", "level": "core", "module": "Core", "seq": 19, "source": "Public post", "takeaway": "A return reason code is a fact about the customer, not a back office label.", "title": "A blurred cheque image, a wrong return code, a customer blamed", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Cheque deposited", "note": "Rs 3,80,000 cheque scanned at the branch."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Image clear?", "note": "A fold leaves the signature area unclear."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Image sent on", "note": "Unclear image enters truncation clearing."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Wrong code used", "note": "Returned as insufficient funds, not unclear image."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Customer blamed", "note": "Charges applied, supplier told cheque bounced."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Correction", "note": "Code fixed, charges reversed, cheque presented again."}]}}, "week": 19, "agenda": "Work through this case: A blurred cheque image, a wrong return code, a customer blamed. A supplier cheque for Rs 3,80,000 was deposited on a Monday. It came back unpaid two days later. The return memo said insufficient funds. The account had held more than enough funds all week. One thing to remember: A return reason code is a fact about the customer, not a back office label.", "thumbnail": "assets/thumbs/case-WC-19.svg"}, {"explain": "Seven monthly debits left the account, each for Rs 12,400.\nThe customer said the mandate had never been signed.\nThe creditor said the mandate was valid and the service had been used.\nThe bank sat between a customer demanding a refund and a creditor demanding payment.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ A direct debit mandate was registered with a mandate reference, and monthly debits began\n→ The customer cancelled the service with the creditor, but the cancellation did not reach the bank as a mandate cancellation\n→ Debits continued for three more months after the customer believed everything had stopped\n→ The customer disputed all seven debits and asked for a refund\n→ The bank refunded the customer and raised an indemnity claim against the creditor bank, which the creditor disputed\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"A registered mandate proves the customer agreed.\"\n\nA registered mandate proves a registration process ran and a reference was created. It does not prove the customer understood the amount, frequency, start date or cancellation terms. Here the record showed the steps taken, but the customer argued the authorisation was presented as a one time verification, not ongoing authority to debit. The bank could produce a record, not a clear account of what was agreed, in words the customer would recognise.\n\nWhy?\n\nA direct debit mandate is an authority from the account holder to the creditor, held through the banks and identified by a mandate reference. It lives or dies on consent and cancellation. The debtor bank debits on its strength, the creditor bank collects for the creditor. When the customer disputes, the protection is deliberately customer facing: refund first, then claim indemnity from the creditor side if the debit was unauthorised. That protects trust, but puts the bank between its customer and a creditor it may also serve.\n\nThe hardest part is cancellation. Cancelling a service is not the same as cancelling a mandate, yet most customers believe it is. If the two are separate, unlinked processes, debits continue after the customer has walked away. A mandate that cannot be cancelled in one place, effective immediately and confirmed in writing, is a dispute waiting for a date. Consent evidence must be retrievable in minutes, not reconstructed over weeks.\n\nA mandate is only as good as the consent evidence behind it.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nMandate design is consent design, tested from the dispute backwards. A BA should ask what evidence is captured at authorisation, how quickly it can be retrieved, and how soon a cancellation stops the next debit. The refund decision needs a rule, not a debate: incomplete evidence means refund and claim indemnity; complete evidence means explain it to the customer, with the evidence shown.\n\nDesign the mandate from the dispute backwards:\n\n→ Consent evidence pack, capturing terms, authentication, date, time and channel at authorisation\n→ One cancellation path, effective immediately, confirmed in writing, from any channel\n→ Refund decision rule, stating when to refund first and claim indemnity afterwards\n→ Evidence retrieval, measured in minutes, with the terms shown in plain words\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Is the mandate registered?\"\n\nIt is \"Can we prove, in minutes, exactly what the customer agreed to, and when it stopped?\"\n\nHave you ever cancelled something, only to watch the debits carry on regardless?", "id": "WC-20", "level": "advanced", "module": "Advanced", "seq": 20, "source": "Public post", "takeaway": "A mandate is only as good as the consent evidence behind it.", "title": "Seven debits of Rs 12,400, and a mandate the customer denied", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "A mandate the customer says was never signed", "parts": [{"label": "Mandate record", "note": "Reference registered, monthly debits begin"}, {"label": "Consent evidence", "note": "Journey shown, understanding not proven"}, {"label": "Cancellation gap", "note": "Service cancelled, mandate never cancelled"}, {"label": "Seven debits", "note": "Rs 12,400 each, three after cancellation belief"}, {"label": "Refund", "note": "Customer refunded first, trust protected"}, {"label": "Indemnity claim", "note": "Bank claims from creditor side, creditor disputes"}]}}, "week": 20, "agenda": "Work through this case: Seven debits of Rs 12,400, and a mandate the customer denied. Seven monthly debits left the account, each for Rs 12,400. The customer said the mandate had never been signed. The creditor said the mandate was valid and the service had been used. The bank sat between a customer demanding a refund and a creditor demanding payment. One thing to remember: A mandate is only as good as the consent evidence behind it.", "thumbnail": "assets/thumbs/case-WC-20.svg"}, {"explain": "A bulk debit file held 12,000 records.\nOne record carried a mandate reference that did not exist.\nThe system rule said reject the whole file.\nRejecting it would delay 11,999 collections that were perfectly valid.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ The corporate customer submitted a bulk file with 12,000 debit records and control totals\n→ Record level validation found one record with an invalid mandate reference\n→ The processing rule offered only two outcomes: process all records, or reject the file\n→ Operations wanted to process the valid records and report the one error, but the system had no partial processing path\n→ The corporate team faced a choice: resubmit the whole file and risk duplicate debits, or lose a day of collections\n→ The file was split manually, the valid records were processed a day late, and the bad record was corrected and resubmitted alone\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"A file with any error is rejected in full.\"\n\nThe rule was written for integrity, and integrity matters. A file is a single instruction, with control totals proving nothing was added, removed or changed. Rejecting the whole file keeps that simple: everything matches, or nothing moves. But simplicity for the system became a blunt outcome for the business. One bad record held up every valid collection and forced a manual split carrying the very risks the rule was meant to prevent.\n\nWhy?\n\nBulk clearing through NACH works at two levels at once. At file level, the sponsor bank checks the file structure, the control totals and the totals that prove the file arrived complete. At record level, each debit is checked against its mandate reference, account details and destination bank, and each record can succeed, fail or be returned independently. The two levels answer different questions: did the file arrive intact, and is each instruction valid. Collapsing record failure into file rejection punishes 11,999 good instructions for one bad one.\n\nPartial processing is not a free choice either. If valid records are processed and the customer resubmits the whole file, they could debit twice unless resubmission is controlled. Control totals must be restated, the audit trail must show what was processed, held and resubmitted, and the customer must agree in advance that partial processing is acceptable. That agreement must be settled before the first bad record arrives, not negotiated on the day.\n\nRejection granularity is a business decision, not a system default.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nFile rejection rules should be agreed, documented and tested with real errors before go live. A BA should ask whether rejection is at file level, record level or both, how errors are reported, and how resubmission is made safe against duplicates. The error report is a customer deliverable: it must name the bad record, state the reason plainly, and say what to do next.\n\nAgree the rejection rule before the bad record arrives:\n\n→ Rejection levels, stating what fails a file and what fails only a record\n→ Error reporting, naming the record, the reason and the corrective action\n→ Control totals, restated correctly for any partial processing\n→ Safe resubmission, with identifiers that prevent duplicate debits\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Do we reject the file?\"\n\nIt is \"Which errors stop everything, which stop one record, and who agreed that in advance?\"\n\nHave you ever seen one bad record hold up thousands of good ones?", "id": "WC-21", "level": "advanced", "module": "Advanced", "seq": 21, "source": "Public post", "takeaway": "Rejection granularity is a business decision, not a system default.", "title": "One bad record in 12,000, and a file rejected whole", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "File received", "note": "12,000 debit records with control totals."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "File checks pass", "note": "Structure and totals prove file arrived intact."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Record check fails", "note": "One mandate reference does not exist."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Reject all or partial?", "note": "System offers only all or nothing."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Manual split", "note": "Valid records processed a day late by hand."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Bad record fixed", "note": "Corrected record resubmitted alone, safely."}]}}, "week": 21, "agenda": "Work through this case: One bad record in 12,000, and a file rejected whole. A bulk debit file held 12,000 records. One record carried a mandate reference that did not exist. The system rule said reject the whole file. Rejecting it would delay 11,999 collections that were perfectly valid. One thing to remember: Rejection granularity is a business decision, not a system default.", "thumbnail": "assets/thumbs/case-WC-21.svg"}, {"explain": "On the last working day of the month, the clearing house went down.\nMillions of items were already queued.\nSalaries, pensions and supplier payments all depended on that day value date.\nWaiting meant missing month-end. The fallback meant settling without the centre.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ The clearing house outage began mid-morning, with no confirmed recovery time\n→ Banks activated a fallback: exchange files bilaterally and settle the resulting positions through RTGS\n→ Without netting, each bank needed far more settlement liquidity\n→ When the clearing house recovered, every fallback item had to be checked against the central queue to prevent double processing\n→ Month-end value dates were protected, but reconciliation took three extra days, and two files needed correction\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The clearing house will always recover before the cut-off.\"\n\nIt usually does, which is why the fallback had never been rehearsed end to end. The playbook, file formats and RTGS path all existed, but nobody had run them together under month-end pressure. The decision to invoke the fallback took nearly an hour, because no role had clear authority to call it, and every bank waited for the others. The fallback worked, slowly and manually, with risks visible only during reconciliation.\n\nWhy?\n\nA clearing house exchanges instructions between banks and nets the obligations, so each bank settles one net position instead of paying every obligation gross. Remove the centre, and both benefits disappear. Bilateral exchange means each pair of banks must agree what was sent, received and owed, with no central record to settle disagreement. Gross RTGS settlement needs far higher liquidity, because the netting benefit is gone and banks must suddenly fund gross outflows. The operational risk rises at the same time as the liquidity risk, which is the worst combination on a month-end day.\n\nThe second risk arrives after recovery. Items settled bilaterally must never also settle centrally, so every fallback item needs a marker and a reconciliation check against the central queue before normal processing resumes. Without that, the fallback that saved month-end creates duplicates the next morning. Fraud and duplicate controls that run centrally must be replicated bilaterally, or the fallback becomes the weakest point at the moment of highest pressure.\n\nA fallback that has never been rehearsed is a hope, not a plan.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nFallback design starts with decision authority and ends with reconciliation. A BA should ask who can invoke the fallback, at what threshold, with what communication, and what liquidity check runs before bilateral settlement begins. The whole playbook must be rehearsed with real volumes, including recovery. A rehearsal that stops at settlement has tested the beginning and ignored the ending.\n\nRehearse the whole fallback, including recovery:\n\n→ Decision authority, naming who calls the fallback and at what threshold\n→ Liquidity check, confirming gross settlement funding before bilateral exchange\n→ Duplicate guards, marking fallback items so they never settle centrally as well\n→ Recovery reconciliation, checking every fallback item before normal processing resumes\n\nThe real requirement is not \"What do we do if the clearing house fails?\"\n\nIt is \"Who decides, how do we settle safely without the centre, and how do we prove nothing was doubled?\"\n\nHave you ever relied on a fallback plan that nobody had actually rehearsed?", "id": "WC-22", "level": "advanced", "module": "Advanced", "seq": 22, "source": "Public post", "takeaway": "A fallback that has never been rehearsed is a hope, not a plan.", "title": "The clearing house stopped on month-end, banks settled alone", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Month-end clearing stops, banks settle bilaterally", "parts": [{"label": "Clearing house", "note": "Outage mid-morning, no confirmed recovery time"}, {"label": "Queued items", "note": "Millions waiting, month-end cut-off approaching"}, {"label": "Bilateral files", "note": "Pairs of banks exchange and match by hand"}, {"label": "RTGS settlement", "note": "Gross positions settle, netting benefit gone"}, {"label": "Liquidity strain", "note": "Far more funding needed without netting"}, {"label": "Recovery check", "note": "Every item checked to prevent double processing"}]}}, "week": 22, "agenda": "Work through this case: The clearing house stopped on month-end, banks settled alone. On the last working day of the month, the clearing house went down. Millions of items were already queued. Salaries, pensions and supplier payments all depended on that day value date. Waiting meant missing month-end. The fallback meant settling without the centre. One thing to remember: A fallback that has never been rehearsed is a hope, not a plan.", "thumbnail": "assets/thumbs/case-WC-22.svg"}, {"explain": "The bank paid its side of a foreign exchange deal on time.\nThe counterparty failed before paying its side.\nThe bank had sent USD 4.2 million and received nothing.\nThe loss was not a market move. It was the full principal.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ The bank agreed to sell USD 4.2 million and buy EUR 3.9 million, same value date\n→ The USD leg was paid through the correspondent bank early in the day, to meet the cut-off\n→ The counterparty failed in the gap, after receiving the USD payment\n→ The EUR leg never arrived, and the bank joined the queue of creditors for the failed counterparty\n→ Payment versus payment would have linked the legs, so neither settled without the other\n→ The deal settled outside that protection to save cost, and one leg was not eligible\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Settlement on the same value date means both legs settle together.\"\n\nSame value date is a calendar promise, not a settlement guarantee. The two legs settle in different systems, currencies and correspondent banks, often hours apart because of time zones and cut-offs. In the gap, the bank that paid first is exposed for the full principal, unsecured. The documents said same day. The reality said one leg in the morning, one in the afternoon, and a failure in between.\n\nWhy?\n\nThis is Herstatt risk, named after a 1974 failure in which one leg was paid and the counterparty failed before the other leg settled. It is principal risk, not replacement cost risk. Failure before settlement costs the rate movement on a replacement deal. Failure after receiving one leg loses the whole amount paid. Time zones lengthen the gap, correspondent banking hides it, and nostro cut-offs force early payment of the first leg. The exposure is intraday and large, and easy to ignore because it usually closes without incident.\n\nPayment versus payment removes the gap by linking the two legs: the USD leg settles only if the EUR leg settles, at the same moment. The protection costs fees and funding discipline, and not every pair, counterparty or value date qualifies. That tempts banks to settle routine looking deals outside the protection. On the rare day it matters, the consequence is loss of the entire principal. Settlement limits and explicit approval for unprotected settlement exist to make that choice visible and deliberate.\n\nIn foreign exchange, paying first without payment versus payment is lending the full principal unsecured.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nForeign exchange settlement must be designed as a risk decision, not just a payment instruction. A BA should ask whether each deal is eligible for payment versus payment settlement, what settlement limit applies to each counterparty, and who approves an exception. For every deal, before the first leg is paid: if the counterparty fails after receiving our leg, what have we lost, and did we choose that exposure knowingly.\n\nMake the exposure visible before the first leg moves:\n\n→ Payment versus payment check, confirming eligibility for every deal before settlement\n→ Settlement limits, capping principal exposure to each counterparty per value date\n→ Exception approval, requiring a named approver for any non protected settlement\n→ Time zone gap analysis, showing the hours between the two legs for each currency pair\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Did both legs settle on the value date?\"\n\nIt is \"What protects the principal in the gap between the two legs?\"\n\nHave you ever paid first and trusted the other leg to follow?", "id": "WC-23", "level": "advanced", "module": "Advanced", "seq": 23, "source": "Public post", "takeaway": "In foreign exchange, paying first without payment versus payment is lending the full principal unsecured.", "title": "USD 4.2 million paid, and the other leg never arrived", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Deal agreed", "note": "Sell USD 4.2 million, buy EUR 3.9 million, same date."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "PvP protected?", "note": "Deal settles outside payment versus payment."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "USD leg paid", "note": "Paid early via correspondent to meet cut-off."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Gap opens", "note": "Hours pass, different time zone, EUR leg pending."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Counterparty fails", "note": "Failure in the gap, after USD was received."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Principal lost", "note": "EUR leg never arrives, full amount at risk."}]}}, "week": 23, "agenda": "Work through this case: USD 4.2 million paid, and the other leg never arrived. The bank paid its side of a foreign exchange deal on time. The counterparty failed before paying its side. The bank had sent USD 4.2 million and received nothing. The loss was not a market move. It was the full principal. One thing to remember: In foreign exchange, paying first without payment versus payment is lending the full principal unsecured.", "thumbnail": "assets/thumbs/case-WC-23.svg"}, {"explain": "The supplier deadline was that evening, and the payment was urgent.\nThe operator chose the cheapest rail, because it said instant.\nThe payment could not reach an overseas account at all.\nBy the time the mistake was clear, hours had been lost.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ The customer needed to pay an overseas supplier USD 18,000 before close of business\n→ The operator selected the domestic instant rail, which showed instant confirmation and a near zero fee\n→ The domestic rail only reaches domestic accounts, so the instruction was rejected\n→ The operator then raised the payment on the gpi rail, with tracking and a transparent fee\n→ The gpi payment carried a unique transaction reference, and the tracker showed each stage to the beneficiary bank\n→ The payment arrived next morning, after the deadline, and the supplier charged a late fee\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Instant on the screen means instant everywhere.\"\n\nBoth rails used the word instant, as if interchangeable. They were not. The domestic rail is fast, cheap and final within its own country and currency, with limits and domestic reach only. The gpi rail crosses borders through correspondent banks, with end to end tracking, transparent fees and exchange, at higher cost and with route cut-offs. Speed without reach is a fast rejection, followed by a slow restart.\n\nWhy?\n\nRail selection trades reach, speed, cost, tracking and finality, and the answer depends on destination, currency, amount and deadline. A domestic instant payment settles in seconds, but cannot settle a foreign currency obligation overseas. A gpi payment can reach the overseas account, and the unique transaction reference lets payer, payer bank and beneficiary bank see the same tracking stages, including fees deducted, but it costs more, needs the right cut-off, and may still arrive the next business day. Fee alone ignores reach. Speed alone ignores cut-offs. Choosing without destination and currency is guessing.\n\nThe interface made the guess easy. It listed rails by fee, showed instant labels without reach warnings, and let an overseas instruction be built on a domestic rail. A better journey asks destination and currency first, shows only rails that can reach, compares cost, arrival and tracking, and warns when the rail cannot meet the deadline. Rail selection is advice, and advice by fee sort order is poor advice.\n\nThe right rail is decided by destination, currency and deadline, not by the fee on the screen.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nRail selection rules should be specified as decision logic, not left to operator habit. A BA should ask what determines eligibility, how destination, currency, amount and deadline are validated, and what comparison the operator sees. Limits, hours and cut-offs matter: a closed, capped or unreachable rail is not an option, however cheap. Tracking needs should drive the choice as much as the fee.\n\nDesign rail selection as a decision, not a list:\n\n→ Eligibility first, showing only rails that reach the destination and currency\n→ Side by side comparison of cost, arrival, tracking and finality\n→ Deadline warning, when the chosen rail cannot meet the deadline\n→ Validation before submission, rejecting unreachable combinations at once\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Which rail is cheapest?\"\n\nIt is \"Which rail can reach this beneficiary, in this currency, by this deadline, with the proof we need?\"\n\nHave you ever chosen the cheapest option and paid far more for the delay?", "id": "WC-24", "level": "core", "module": "Core", "seq": 24, "source": "Public post", "takeaway": "The right rail is decided by destination, currency and deadline, not by the fee on the screen.", "title": "An urgent USD 18,000 supplier payment took the wrong rail", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Two rails called instant, only one could reach", "parts": [{"label": "Domestic instant", "note": "Seconds, near zero fee, domestic accounts only"}, {"label": "gpi cross-border", "note": "Reaches overseas, tracked, higher fee, cut-offs apply"}, {"label": "Cost", "note": "Fee saved was a fraction of the late fee charged"}, {"label": "Speed", "note": "Fast rejection, then a slow restart on the right rail"}, {"label": "Reach", "note": "Destination and currency decide which rails qualify"}, {"label": "Tracking", "note": "Unique reference shows each stage to the beneficiary"}]}}, "week": 24, "agenda": "Work through this case: An urgent USD 18,000 supplier payment took the wrong rail. The supplier deadline was that evening, and the payment was urgent. The operator chose the cheapest rail, because it said instant. The payment could not reach an overseas account at all. By the time the mistake was clear, hours had been lost. One thing to remember: The right rail is decided by destination, currency and deadline, not by the fee on the screen.", "thumbnail": "assets/thumbs/case-WC-24.svg"}, {"explain": "The migration went live on a Monday, with structured addresses.\nBy Tuesday the repair queue held 1,800 payments.\nStraight-through processing fell by a third in a single day.\nThe messages were correct. The systems downstream were not ready.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ The bank began sending pacs.008 payments with structured postal addresses\n→ Structured addresses split free text into town, postcode, street, building, country\n→ A legacy screening system expected unstructured lines, and rejected the new format\n→ The core system truncated structured fields on storage, losing the building number\n→ Each rejected payment landed in the repair queue, rebuilt by hand\n→ The bank paused the rollout, mapped fields for legacy systems, and cleared the queue in nine days\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Send structured addresses from the migration date.\"\n\nThe requirement described the sending system only. It said nothing about screening, core banking, reconciliation or reporting, all consuming the same address data in the old format. None had been tested with real structured data at volume. The team had tested that messages could be created, not that the bank could process them end to end. The repair queue became the test environment, with live payments as test data.\n\nWhy?\n\nISO 20022 allows structured addresses, with separate elements for street, building, town, postcode and country, unstructured free text lines, and hybrid combinations. Structured addresses improve data quality, screening and straight-through processing, but only when every system in the chain can read the new form. A screening system expecting address lines cannot screen a structured address it cannot parse, and a core system with short fixed fields cannot store new elements. Truncation, rejection and manual repair follow, and repair at volume adds inconsistency, delay and screening decisions made on rebuilt data.\n\nThe failure was a scope failure, not a message failure. Message migration, data remediation, downstream mapping and repair capacity are one programme, and go live must wait until all four are ready. Structured addresses are only as good as the beneficiary data behind them, much of it free text never split into elements. A phased approach with hybrid addresses would have cost weeks of preparation and saved nine days of firefighting.\n\nA migration is finished when every downstream system can read the new format, not when the first message goes out.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nMigration requirements must be written end to end, from creation to posting and reporting. A BA should ask which systems consume each changed field, what each expects, and how the new format maps to the old. Data remediation needs an owner, repair capacity needs a forecast, and testing needs real address variants, including long names and missing postcodes. A pause criterion agreed before go live turns a bad first day into a controlled pause.\n\nMigrate the chain, not just the message:\n\n→ Downstream impact analysis, listing every consumer\n→ Field mapping, showing how new elements map to legacy expectations\n→ Data remediation, cleaning beneficiary address data before go live\n→ Repair capacity plan, forecasting volumes and staffing for exceptions\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Can we send the new format?\"\n\nIt is \"Can every system downstream read, store, screen and report on it, at volume?\"\n\nHave you ever watched a migration succeed in the sending system and fail everywhere else?", "id": "WC-25", "level": "advanced", "module": "Advanced", "seq": 25, "source": "Public post", "takeaway": "A migration is finished when every downstream system can read the new format, not when the first message goes out.", "title": "A structured address the legacy system could not read at all", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Structured address sent", "note": "pacs.008 carries town, postcode, street, number, country."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Downstream ready?", "note": "Screening expects unstructured lines, core truncates."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Rejections pile up", "note": "1,800 payments land in the repair queue."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Manual repair", "note": "Operators rebuild addresses inconsistently, under pressure."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Rollout paused", "note": "Mapping fixed for legacy systems, queue cleared."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Lesson learned", "note": "Migrate the chain, not just the message."}]}}, "week": 25, "agenda": "Work through this case: A structured address the legacy system could not read at all. The migration went live on a Monday, with structured addresses. By Tuesday the repair queue held 1,800 payments. Straight-through processing fell by a third in a single day. The messages were correct. The systems downstream were not ready. One thing to remember: A migration is finished when every downstream system can read the new format, not when the first message goes out.", "thumbnail": "assets/thumbs/case-WC-25.svg"}, {"explain": "The instruction said EUR 250,000.\nThe settlement account was debited in USD 250,000.\nThe numbers matched. The currency did not.\nBy then it had settled in central bank money.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ An operator keyed a payment instruction for EUR 250,000 to a beneficiary bank\n→ The currency code was entered as USD, and 250,000 was left unchanged\n→ Validation checked amount, account and beneficiary, but not the currency\n→ The payment settled through the central bank in USD, from the USD account\n→ The beneficiary bank received USD, converted it, and credited the beneficiary short\n→ Exchange movement between the value dates left a loss, disputed by both banks\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"If the amount is right, the payment is right.\"\n\nThe amount was right, and the payment was wrong, because an amount without its currency is not an amount at all. The number 250,000 means one value in euro, another in dollars, with a different settlement account and funding check in each case. Validation checked every field except the relationship between them: currency against instruction, account and beneficiary expectation. Each field was valid alone. Together they described a payment nobody had instructed.\n\nWhy?\n\nCurrency codes follow ISO 4217, three letter codes such as EUR, USD and INR, and settlement systems hold separate accounts and liquidity per currency. An instruction carries amount and currency as a pair, and account, funding check and credit must all use the same pair. When the code is wrong, the payment still passes every single field check, because USD, 250,000 and the beneficiary account are each valid alone. Only a cross-field check catches the error. Central bank settlement is final, so the sending bank cannot undo the error alone. It needs a return, beneficiary bank agreement, a fresh payment and a new value date, with any exchange movement in between becoming a loss someone must bear.\n\nThe control failure is human as much as technical. Currency is a small field, entered early, easily overlooked in a four eyes check focused on amount and beneficiary name. Free text entry, or a dropdown defaulting to the most used currency, invites this error. A code list with no default, a confirmation showing amount and currency together, and separate checker confirmation would each have caught it.\n\nCurrency is part of the amount, so a payment with the wrong currency is a wrong payment, even when the number matches.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nValidation rules must check relationships between fields, not just fields in isolation. A BA should ask which cross-field checks exist, how currency is entered, whether any default applies, and what the maker checker process confirms. High value payments deserve a confirmation stating amount, currency, settlement account and beneficiary together, forcing the checker to read the pair, not just the number. The reversal playbook must be ready before the error happens.\n\nCheck the pair, not just the number:\n\n→ Cross-field validation, matching currency to instruction and account\n→ No default currency, so the code is chosen deliberately every time\n→ Separate checker confirmation of the currency, explicitly\n→ Reversal playbook, covering return, fresh payment and loss ownership\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Is the amount correct?\"\n\nIt is \"Do amount, currency and settlement account describe the same payment?\"\n\nHave you ever seen a small field error create a large payment problem?", "id": "WC-26", "level": "core", "module": "Core", "seq": 26, "source": "Public post", "takeaway": "Currency is part of the amount, so a payment with the wrong currency is a wrong payment, even when the number matches.", "title": "250,000 settled at the central bank, in the wrong currency", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "250,000 paid, in the wrong currency", "parts": [{"label": "Instruction", "note": "EUR 250,000 to the beneficiary bank"}, {"label": "Currency code", "note": "Entered as USD, amount left unchanged"}, {"label": "Settlement account", "note": "USD account debited, funding check passed"}, {"label": "Central bank settlement", "note": "Final settlement, cannot be undone alone"}, {"label": "Beneficiary credit", "note": "USD converted, beneficiary credited short"}, {"label": "Reversal", "note": "Return, fresh EUR payment, new value date, loss disputed"}]}}, "week": 26, "agenda": "Work through this case: 250,000 settled at the central bank, in the wrong currency. The instruction said EUR 250,000. The settlement account was debited in USD 250,000. The numbers matched. The currency did not. By then it had settled in central bank money. One thing to remember: Currency is part of the amount, so a payment with the wrong currency is a wrong payment, even when the number matches.", "thumbnail": "assets/thumbs/case-WC-26.svg"}, {"explain": "A customer sent EUR 18,500 to a supplier overseas.\nThe money left the account on a Monday.\nBy Friday, the supplier said nothing had arrived.\nThe customer called every day after that.\nNine days passed, and nobody could say where the money was.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ pacs.008 sent to the beneficiary bank\n→ pacs.002 confirmed the instruction was accepted\n→ pacs.028 sent to ask for the payment status\n→ No pacs.029 came back\n→ A second pacs.028 was sent, and still nothing\n→ The customer escalated daily, and each time heard the same answer\n→ On day nine, the case was still open, with no new information\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"If we send an investigation, the other bank will answer.\"\n\nBut an investigation request is a question, not an order. The beneficiary bank is under no technical obligation to reply within any fixed time, and no system forces an answer. So the team waited, the customer waited, and the case aged in silence.\n\nWhy?\n\npacs.028 asks for a status update, and pacs.029 carries the answer. Between the two there is a gap that no message fills: the time, the priority and the ownership on the receiving side. A pacs.028 can sit in another bank's investigation queue for days while its own cut-offs, volumes and staffing decide when it gets looked at. Sending the same request again does not move it up that queue, and it does not create any new evidence about the payment itself.\n\nThe deeper problem is that the bank's own process treated sending the request as progress. The case record showed the pacs.028 as sent, so the case looked active. In reality nothing had changed since day one. The customer was not asking for a message to be sent. The customer was asking where the money was, and the bank had no answer, no deadline and no escalation path beyond waiting for a reply that might never come. Each escalation call created a new note and a new promise, but none of them changed the state of the investigation. The bank was busy, the customer was angry, and the payment was exactly where it had been on day one: unconfirmed.\n\nAn investigation sent is not an investigation answered.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nDesign investigations for silence, not just for replies. Every pacs.028 needs an ageing clock, a chase date, an escalation step and a customer update, even when the update is that there is nothing new. A case that only moves when a pacs.029 arrives will stall exactly when the customer needs it most. The bank cannot control the other bank's reply time. What it can control is its own clock, its own escalation and its own communication, and those are the parts the customer actually experiences.\n\nBuild these into the investigation flow:\n\n→ An ageing timer that starts the day pacs.028 goes out\n→ A chase step with a named owner, not just a repeat message\n→ An escalation route beyond messaging, through the relationship or correspondent channel\n→ A customer update on a fixed rhythm, even when there is no new information\n→ A closure rule that needs evidence of the payment outcome, not just a reply\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Can we send a pacs.028?\"\n\nIt is \"What happens when nobody answers, and who owns the silence?\"\n\nHave you ever watched a case age while everyone waited for a reply that never came?", "id": "WC-27", "level": "core", "module": "Core", "seq": 27, "source": "Public post", "takeaway": "An investigation sent is not an investigation answered.", "title": "Nine days, one pacs.028, and no pacs.029: the payment nobody could find", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "pacs.028 sent, no pacs.029, nine days of silence", "parts": [{"label": "pacs.028", "note": "The status request that went out, and changed nothing on its own"}, {"label": "pacs.029", "note": "The answer that never arrived, with no deadline forcing it"}, {"label": "Ageing clock", "note": "The timer that should have started the day the request went out"}, {"label": "Escalation route", "note": "The path beyond messaging, through the relationship channel"}, {"label": "Customer updates", "note": "The fixed rhythm of contact, even when there is nothing new"}, {"label": "Evidence of outcome", "note": "The proof that the payment arrived, without which the case stays open"}]}}, "week": 27, "agenda": "Work through this case: Nine days, one pacs.028, and no pacs.029: the payment nobody could find. A customer sent EUR 18,500 to a supplier overseas. The money left the account on a Monday. By Friday, the supplier said nothing had arrived. The customer called every day after that. Nine days passed, and nobody could say where the money was. One thing to remember: An investigation sent is not an investigation answered.", "thumbnail": "assets/thumbs/case-WC-27.svg"}, {"explain": "A corporate customer sent EUR 40,000 to the wrong supplier account.\nThe error was spotted within the hour.\nA camt.056 recall was sent the same morning.\nThe beneficiary bank replied that the funds had already been withdrawn.\nThe money was gone, and the recall had nothing left to recall.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ pacs.008 sent with the wrong beneficiary account\n→ The error was found and a camt.056 recall was sent\n→ The beneficiary bank checked the account\n→ The funds had already been withdrawn in cash\n→ No pacs.004 return could be made, because there was nothing to return\n→ The customer asked the bank to recover the money anyway\n→ The bank had to explain that a recall is a request, not a guarantee\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"A recall sent quickly will always get the money back.\"\n\nSpeed helps, but it does not change what a recall is. A camt.056 asks the beneficiary bank to return the funds. It can only succeed if the funds are still there, and if the beneficiary bank can debit the account. Once the money has been withdrawn, the recall has no funds to act on, and the conversation changes from recovery of a payment to recovery of a debt.\n\nWhy?\n\nThe distinction matters because it decides who bears the loss and what happens next. If the error was the customer's, the bank's duty is to try, to evidence the attempt, and to be honest about the outcome. If the error was the bank's, the customer has a much stronger claim, and the bank may need to make the customer whole while it pursues recovery itself. Either way, the recall message does not decide the outcome. The state of the beneficiary account does, and the consent of the beneficiary matters the moment a debit is proposed.\n\nThe relationship question is harder than the message flow. The customer did everything right after the error: spotted it fast, reported it fast, and expected the bank's speed to match. Telling that customer that the recall failed feels like the bank giving up, even when the bank did everything the process allows. The real work is in the conversation that follows: what recovery steps remain, what legal routes exist, what the bank will do next, and when the customer will hear about it. A recall that fails silently, with no next step and no owner, damages the relationship far more than an honest account of a failed recall.\n\nA recall asks for the money back. It does not bring it back.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nWhen you specify recall handling, specify the failure path as carefully as the success path. A recall can end in a return, a partial return, a refusal, or no funds at all, and each outcome needs its own customer wording, its own next step and its own owner. The happy path is the easy part. The failed recall is where the customer judges the bank.\n\nCover these in the recall flow:\n\n→ A funds availability check at the beneficiary bank, before any promise is made\n→ A clear split between customer error and bank error, because it decides who bears the loss\n→ A recovery plan for the no funds outcome, with legal and relationship steps\n→ Customer wording that explains a recall is a request, without sounding like an excuse\n→ An owner and a date for the next update, even when the recall has failed\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Can we send a camt.056?\"\n\nIt is \"What do we do, and what do we say, when the recall comes back empty?\"\n\nHave you ever had to tell a customer that a fast recall still arrived too late?", "id": "WC-28", "level": "advanced", "module": "Advanced", "seq": 28, "source": "Public post", "takeaway": "A recall asks for the money back. It does not bring it back.", "title": "The camt.056 recall arrived, but the money had already been withdrawn", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Error spotted", "note": "The wrong beneficiary account is found within the hour."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Recall sent", "note": "A camt.056 recall goes out the same morning."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Funds still there?", "note": "The beneficiary bank checks whether anything is left to return."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "No funds", "note": "The money was withdrawn, so no pacs.004 return is possible."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Recovery plan", "note": "Legal and relationship steps replace the failed recall."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Customer conversation", "note": "An honest account, a named owner, and a date for the next update."}]}}, "week": 28, "agenda": "Work through this case: The camt.056 recall arrived, but the money had already been withdrawn. A corporate customer sent EUR 40,000 to the wrong supplier account. The error was spotted within the hour. A camt.056 recall was sent the same morning. The beneficiary bank replied that the funds had already been withdrawn. The money was gone, and the recall had nothing left to recall. One thing to remember: A recall asks for the money back. It does not bring it back.", "thumbnail": "assets/thumbs/case-WC-28.svg"}, {"explain": "A customer was tricked into sending GBP 22,000 to a fraudster.\nThe payment was authorised by the customer, in the bank's app.\nBy the time the customer realised it was a scam, the money had moved on.\nThe bank was asked to recall it.\nThe beneficiary account was already empty.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ The customer authorised a payment to an account controlled by a fraudster\n→ pacs.008 settled, and the funds were credited to the beneficiary account\n→ The funds were withdrawn within minutes, before any warning arrived\n→ The customer reported the scam and asked for a recall\n→ A camt.056 recall was sent, citing fraud as the reason\n→ The beneficiary bank confirmed there were no funds left to return\n→ The customer asked why the bank had let the payment go through at all\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"If the payment was a scam, the bank can simply take the money back.\"\n\nBut an authorised push payment is not an unauthorised transaction. The customer instructed it, authenticated it, and the bank executed a valid instruction. A recall can ask for the funds back, but it cannot undo the authorisation, and it cannot create funds that have already left the account.\n\nWhy?\n\nThis is the hardest case in payments operations, because every party did what the system asked of them and the outcome is still a loss. The customer was deceived, not careless in the way the word is usually meant. The bank executed a properly authorised instruction. The beneficiary bank received funds into an account that, at that moment, looked ordinary. The fraudster understood the speed of the system better than anyone else in the chain, and used it. Speed is the feature that makes modern payments useful, and it is also the feature that makes recovery nearly impossible once the money has moved.\n\nThe lesson is not that recalls are useless. A fast recall sometimes catches funds that have not yet moved, and it always creates the evidence trail that later recovery and reimbursement decisions depend on. The lesson is that prevention, warning and confirmation at the moment of payment matter far more than any recall sent afterwards. A warning before confirmation, a check on a new payee, a pause on a first payment to an unfamiliar account: these controls change the outcome. A recall is the last line of defence, and by the time it is needed, the money is usually already gone.\n\nA recall after the money is gone is recovery, not reversal.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nWhen you work on fraud and recall flows, keep three things separate: the payment instruction, which was valid; the deception, which happened outside the payment system; and the recovery, which depends on funds still being present. Mixing them up produces requirements that promise reversals the system cannot deliver, and customer wording that sounds like blame.\n\nBuild the flow around these:\n\n→ A fraud recall path that moves in minutes, with the fraud reason clearly coded\n→ Evidence capture from the first report, because reimbursement decisions need it\n→ Honest customer wording about what a recall can and cannot do\n→ Prevention controls at payment time, where the outcome can still change\n→ A victim care path with a named owner, not just a case number\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Can we recall a scam payment?\"\n\nIt is \"What stops the payment before it leaves, and what do we do for the victim when it does not?\"\n\nHave you ever seen a recall treated as the fraud control, when the real control had to happen before the payment?", "id": "WC-29", "level": "advanced", "module": "Advanced", "seq": 29, "source": "Public post", "takeaway": "A recall after the money is gone is recovery, not reversal.", "title": "The scam victim asked for a recall, and the account was already empty", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Authorised payment, stolen outcome, empty account", "parts": [{"label": "Authorisation", "note": "The customer instructed and authenticated a valid payment"}, {"label": "Speed of settlement", "note": "The feature that makes the payment useful, and recovery nearly impossible"}, {"label": "camt.056 fraud recall", "note": "The request that went out in minutes, and found nothing left"}, {"label": "Empty account", "note": "The funds were withdrawn before any warning arrived"}, {"label": "Prevention controls", "note": "Warnings and checks at payment time, where the outcome can still change"}, {"label": "Victim care", "note": "A named owner and an evidence trail, not just a case number"}]}}, "week": 29, "agenda": "Work through this case: The scam victim asked for a recall, and the account was already empty. A customer was tricked into sending GBP 22,000 to a fraudster. The payment was authorised by the customer, in the bank's app. By the time the customer realised it was a scam, the money had moved on. The bank was asked to recall it. The beneficiary account was already empty. One thing to remember: A recall after the money is gone is recovery, not reversal.", "thumbnail": "assets/thumbs/case-WC-29.svg"}, {"explain": "A payment of EUR 25,000 was credited to the wrong account.\nThe intended beneficiary never received it.\nThe account that received it belonged to a different customer of the same bank.\nThe operations team asked for the funds to be debited back.\nThe answer was no, not without the account holder's consent.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ pacs.008 was sent with an incorrect beneficiary account number\n→ The funds were credited to the wrong account\n→ The error was discovered the same day\n→ The bank asked the account holder to agree to a debit\n→ The account holder did not respond\n→ The intended beneficiary escalated, and the sending customer demanded the money back\n→ The funds stayed where they were, because no one could lawfully move them\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"If the bank made the error, the bank can simply reverse it.\"\n\nA credit to an account creates a balance that belongs to the account holder, even when the credit was a mistake. The bank cannot help itself to that balance without consent, a court order, or a specific legal basis. The error explains what happened. It does not authorise the debit.\n\nWhy?\n\nThis surprises people because it feels backwards. The bank can see the error, can prove the error, and can show exactly where the money went, yet it cannot fix it by moving the money back. The reason is the same protection that stops a bank from debiting any account whenever it believes it has a reason. Consent to debit is a control that protects every customer, and it does not switch off because the bank is the one asking. The account holder may have spent the funds, may dispute the claim, or may simply not answer, and each of those situations needs a different route.\n\nThe practical consequence is that the bank needs two tracks at once. One track pursues the return: contact the account holder, request consent, explain the error, and escalate through the proper legal route if consent is refused. The other track looks after the customer who lost the money: acknowledge the error, explain what is happening and when, and decide whether the bank will make the customer whole while recovery is pursued. The worst outcome is to promise a quick reversal, discover the consent problem, and then go silent while the customer waits for money that is not coming back quickly.\n\nCrediting the wrong account does not give the bank the right to debit it.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nNever specify a reversal as a simple system action when a customer account is involved. A debit from an account needs authority, and the system must record what that authority is: consent, a court order, or the terms that permit it. A reverse button that moves money without that evidence is a control failure waiting for an audit.\n\nRequire these in the error correction flow:\n\n→ A consent request to the account holder, with a record of the response\n→ A hold on any debit until authority is evidenced, not just assumed\n→ A separate track for the affected customer, with its own updates\n→ An escalation route when consent is refused or never arrives\n→ A clear decision on who bears the loss while recovery is pursued\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Can we reverse the credit?\"\n\nIt is \"What authority allows this debit, and what do we do when we do not have it?\"\n\nHave you ever seen a simple reversal blocked by a consent rule nobody had specified?", "id": "WC-30", "level": "core", "module": "Core", "seq": 30, "source": "Public post", "takeaway": "Crediting the wrong account does not give the bank the right to debit it.", "title": "The wrong person was credited EUR 25,000, and the bank could not take it back", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Wrong credit", "note": "EUR 25,000 lands in an account that was never meant to receive it."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Error discovered", "note": "The mistake is found the same day, with full proof of where the money went."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Consent to debit?", "note": "The account holder must agree, or a lawful basis must exist."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Debit with authority", "note": "The return proceeds only once consent or an order is evidenced."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Recovery without consent", "note": "Contact, explanation, and the proper legal route when consent never arrives."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Customer made whole", "note": "A separate decision on who bears the loss while recovery is pursued."}]}}, "week": 30, "agenda": "Work through this case: The wrong person was credited EUR 25,000, and the bank could not take it back. A payment of EUR 25,000 was credited to the wrong account. The intended beneficiary never received it. The account that received it belonged to a different customer of the same bank. The operations team asked for the funds to be debited back. The answer was no, not without the account holder's consent. One thing to remember: Crediting the wrong account does not give the bank the right to debit it.", "thumbnail": "assets/thumbs/case-WC-30.svg"}, {"explain": "A corporate customer uploaded a bulk payment file on a Monday morning.\nThe file contained 240 salary payments.\nThe upload timed out, so the customer uploaded it again.\nBoth uploads were processed.\nEvery employee was paid twice, and the customer discovered it from its own staff.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ The bulk file was uploaded through the corporate channel\n→ The first upload timed out, but the file had already been accepted\n→ The customer uploaded the same file a second time\n→ No duplicate check stopped the second file\n→ Both files were processed, and 480 payments went out\n→ The customer account was debited twice for the full amount\n→ Recovery began only after employees reported the double payment\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Process every file the customer uploads.\"\n\nThe requirement was satisfied, twice. Nothing in the flow asked whether the file had been seen before, whether the batch reference was already in use, or whether the same payments had already been instructed. The system did exactly what it was told, and that was the problem.\n\nWhy?\n\nDuplicate detection is an identity problem before it is a technical problem. A file needs an identity that survives a timeout, a retry and a re-upload: a file hash, a batch reference, or a combination of customer, value date and control totals that makes the same file recognisable the second time it arrives. Individual payments need the same treatment, because a file can be re-uploaded under a new name while the payments inside it stay identical. Without identity at both levels, the system cannot tell a genuine second file from an accidental repeat, and it will process both.\n\nThe timeout is the detail that makes this case common. The customer did the reasonable thing: the screen gave no confirmation, so the customer tried again. A flow that accepts a file and then fails to confirm it invites a duplicate, and a flow with no duplicate check guarantees one. Recovery after a double payment is slow, embarrassing and expensive, because it needs the consent of every person who was overpaid, and some of them will already have spent the money. Prevention costs a hash check and a confirmation screen. Recovery costs weeks.\n\nA file processed twice is not a duplicate. It is two payments.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nSpecify duplicate protection as a first class requirement, not as an edge case. Every bulk intake needs a file identity check, a payment identity check, and a confirmation that survives a timeout. The customer must never be left guessing whether an upload worked, because guessing is what creates the second upload.\n\nInsist on these controls:\n\n→ A file hash or batch reference check that rejects a repeat of the same file\n→ Payment level identity, so the same instruction cannot settle twice under a new file name\n→ A confirmation the customer can rely on, even when the upload times out\n→ A warning, not a silent accept, when a file looks like a near duplicate\n→ A recovery plan for double payments, including the consent problem for each overpaid account\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Can we process bulk files quickly?\"\n\nIt is \"How do we know this file has not already been processed, and how does the customer know it worked?\"\n\nHave you ever seen a timeout create a duplicate that no control caught?", "id": "WC-31", "level": "advanced", "module": "Advanced", "seq": 31, "source": "Public post", "takeaway": "A file processed twice is not a duplicate. It is two payments.", "title": "The same bulk file was paid twice, and nobody noticed until the second debit", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "One file, two processings, 480 payments", "parts": [{"label": "File hash", "note": "The identity that would have recognised the same file the second time"}, {"label": "Batch reference", "note": "The reference that should never be accepted twice"}, {"label": "Timeout", "note": "The missing confirmation that made a reasonable customer upload again"}, {"label": "Confirmation", "note": "The proof the customer needed that the first upload had worked"}, {"label": "Payment identity", "note": "The check that stops the same instruction settling under a new file name"}, {"label": "Recovery", "note": "The slow consent based recovery from every person overpaid"}]}}, "week": 31, "agenda": "Work through this case: The same bulk file was paid twice, and nobody noticed until the second debit. A corporate customer uploaded a bulk payment file on a Monday morning. The file contained 240 salary payments. The upload timed out, so the customer uploaded it again. Both uploads were processed. Every employee was paid twice, and the customer discovered it from its own staff. One thing to remember: A file processed twice is not a duplicate. It is two payments.", "thumbnail": "assets/thumbs/case-WC-31.svg"}, {"explain": "A corporate treasurer called on a Tuesday morning.\nA payment of EUR 2,000,000 should have gone out on Monday, but it had not.\nThe treasurer asked for the value date to be set to Monday.\nThe difference was one day of interest, and a covenant report that would look different.\nOne manager had to decide whether to agree.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ The payment was instructed late and missed the Monday value date\n→ The treasurer requested a Monday value date on a Tuesday instruction\n→ Backdating would change the interest calculation on the account\n→ It would also change the figures in a report already shared with a lender\n→ The operations team checked the policy and found no clear rule\n→ The relationship manager pressed for the change, citing the client relationship\n→ The decision sat with one manager, with no audit trail for either answer\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The value date is just a field, and the customer is asking us to correct it.\"\n\nA value date is not a label. It is the date from which interest is calculated, the date the funds are available, and the date that feeds reporting. Changing it after the fact rewrites all three, silently if the system keeps no trace of the original date.\n\nWhy?\n\nThe ethical line is easy to state and hard to hold in the moment. If the bank caused the delay, a value date correction with full disclosure and an audit trail is a legitimate remedy, and the customer should not pay for the bank's error. If the customer caused the delay, backdating transfers the cost of that delay to the bank, and changes a report that a third party relies on. The treasurer's request sits exactly on that line, and the pressure to agree comes from the relationship, not from the facts. A control that depends on one manager resisting that pressure, with no policy, is not a control.\n\nThe right answer is rarely a silent edit. Where a correction is justified, it should be visible: the original value date retained, the correction recorded with its reason and its approver, and any interest effect shown as a separate, transparent adjustment. Where it is not justified, the answer is a clear no, with the reason explained and the alternative offered. What must never happen is a quiet change to a date field that leaves no evidence that anything changed, because that is the change an audit, a regulator or a lender will find later, and by then it looks like concealment even when it started as helpfulness.\n\nA value date is a fact about when money moved, not a field to be edited.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nWhen you specify value date handling, specify who can change it, when, with whose approval, and what evidence is kept. A value date field that any authorised user can edit, with no reason code and no history, is an invitation to the exact request in this case. The control is not the refusal. The control is the trail.\n\nRequire these in the design:\n\n→ A rule that separates bank caused delay from customer caused delay\n→ A correction path that keeps the original value date visible\n→ A reason code and an approver for every value date change\n→ Interest effects shown as separate adjustments, never hidden in the date\n→ A policy the operations team can point to, so one manager never decides alone\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Can we change the value date?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves this date is true, and who approved it if it changed?\"\n\nHave you ever been asked to backdate something that looked small and turned out not to be?", "id": "WC-32", "level": "advanced", "module": "Advanced", "seq": 32, "source": "Public post", "takeaway": "A value date is a fact about when money moved, not a field to be edited.", "title": "The treasurer asked for yesterday's value date, and the answer was not simple", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Request to backdate", "note": "A Monday value date is asked for on a Tuesday instruction."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Who caused the delay?", "note": "Bank caused delay and customer caused delay lead to different answers."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Transparent correction", "note": "Original date retained, reason and approver recorded, interest shown separately."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Refusal with reason", "note": "A clear no, with the reason explained and an alternative offered."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Audit trail", "note": "Every change evidenced, so no edit is ever silent."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Interest adjustment", "note": "The interest effect shown as its own adjustment, never hidden in the date."}]}}, "week": 32, "agenda": "Work through this case: The treasurer asked for yesterday's value date, and the answer was not simple. A corporate treasurer called on a Tuesday morning. A payment of EUR 2,000,000 should have gone out on Monday, but it had not. The treasurer asked for the value date to be set to Monday. The difference was one day of interest, and a covenant report that would look different. One manager had to decide whether to agree. One thing to remember: A value date is a fact about when money moved, not a field to be edited.", "thumbnail": "assets/thumbs/case-WC-32.svg"}, {"explain": "A customer sent EUR 12,000 to a supplier.\nA pacs.002 status report came back saying the payment was rejected.\nThe customer sent the payment again.\nThe supplier was paid twice.\nThe first payment had never been rejected at all. The debit had already happened.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ pacs.008 was sent, and the customer account was debited\n→ A pacs.002 arrived with a rejected status\n→ The customer screen showed the payment as rejected\n→ The customer instructed the payment a second time\n→ The second payment also settled\n→ The supplier received EUR 24,000 for a EUR 12,000 invoice\n→ Reconciliation showed the first debit had never been reversed\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The pacs.002 status is the truth about the payment.\"\n\nA pacs.002 is a report about a payment, generated by a system that may itself be wrong, late, or out of sync with the ledger. The ledger is the truth about money movement. When the report and the ledger disagree, the report is the thing to question, not the money.\n\nWhy?\n\nStatus and settlement are two different facts, produced by two different systems, and they can diverge in several ordinary ways. A status message can be generated before settlement completes, or after a retry, or from a cached state that no longer matches the account. A mapping error can turn an accepted status into a rejected one on the way to the screen. A duplicate status report can overwrite the correct one. None of these are exotic failures. They are the everyday consequences of a chain in which the payment, the ledger and the status travel on different paths at different speeds.\n\nThe customer behaviour that follows is entirely reasonable. Told that a payment was rejected, a customer sends it again, because the supplier is waiting and the screen says the first attempt failed. The system that shows a rejected status next to a completed debit is not just displaying wrong information. It is actively causing a duplicate payment. The fix is not to make the customer check the account balance before re-sending. The fix is to make the status derive from the same evidence as the ledger, and to reconcile the two continuously, so that a rejected status can never sit alongside a settled debit without an alert.\n\nA status report describes the ledger. It does not replace it.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nNever specify a status display that trusts a single message. The status a customer sees must be reconciled against the ledger, and any disagreement must surface as an exception, not as a confident label. A payment can be in exactly one state, and the system must know which evidence decides that state.\n\nBuild these checks into the status flow:\n\n→ A reconciliation between pacs.002 status and the ledger, running continuously\n→ An exception when a rejected status meets a settled debit, before the customer sees it\n→ A rule that blocks a re-instruction while the first payment is unreconciled\n→ A status vocabulary that distinguishes rejected, unconfirmed and unknown\n→ An audit of status changes, so a wrong status can be traced to its source\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Can we show the pacs.002 status?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves this status, and what happens when the evidence disagrees?\"\n\nHave you ever trusted a status message that the ledger contradicted?", "id": "WC-33", "level": "core", "module": "Core", "seq": 33, "source": "Public post", "takeaway": "A status report describes the ledger. It does not replace it.", "title": "The pacs.002 said rejected, but the debit had already happened", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "pacs.002 says rejected, the ledger says settled", "parts": [{"label": "pacs.008", "note": "The instruction that was sent and acted on"}, {"label": "Ledger debit", "note": "The truth about money movement, already completed"}, {"label": "pacs.002", "note": "The report that said rejected, and was wrong"}, {"label": "Customer screen", "note": "The display that showed rejected and caused a second payment"}, {"label": "Duplicate payment", "note": "EUR 24,000 paid for a EUR 12,000 invoice"}, {"label": "Reconciliation", "note": "The continuous check that should have caught the disagreement first"}]}}, "week": 33, "agenda": "Work through this case: The pacs.002 said rejected, but the debit had already happened. A customer sent EUR 12,000 to a supplier. A pacs.002 status report came back saying the payment was rejected. The customer sent the payment again. The supplier was paid twice. The first payment had never been rejected at all. The debit had already happened. One thing to remember: A status report describes the ledger. It does not replace it.", "thumbnail": "assets/thumbs/case-WC-33.svg"}, {"explain": "A customer reported a missing payment of EUR 9,800.\nAn investigation case was opened, and a pacs.028 was sent.\nThree days later, the case was marked resolved.\nThe customer had not been paid, had not been contacted, and had not agreed to anything.\nThe money was still missing. The queue simply looked better.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ The customer reported the payment as missing\n→ An investigation case was opened with a target closure date\n→ A pacs.028 was sent to the beneficiary bank\n→ No pacs.029 had arrived when the target date approached\n→ The case was closed as resolved, to meet the target\n→ The customer called, and was told the case was closed\n→ A new case had to be opened, and the investigation started again\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Close investigation cases within three working days.\"\n\nThe target was met, and the customer was failed. The closure recorded an action taken, not an outcome achieved, and the system accepted it because nothing in the closure step asked for evidence that the money had been found.\n\nWhy?\n\nClosure criteria are where operational targets meet customer reality, and this case shows what happens when the target wins. A case closed on the basis of a message sent, a task completed, or a date reached is a case closed on activity, not on outcome. The customer does not experience activity. The customer experiences the money arriving, or not arriving, and a closure that happens before that moment is a fiction the system tells itself. Worse, it destroys the trail: the new case starts from scratch, the history is split, and the customer has to tell the story again to someone new.\n\nThe pressure behind premature closure is real. Queues are measured, teams are judged on ageing, and an open case past its target looks like failure. But a closed case with missing money is not success, it is a hidden failure, and hidden failures cost more than visible ones. They generate repeat contacts, complaints, and the loss of trust that follows when a customer is told a problem is resolved that plainly is not. The honest measure is not how fast cases close. It is how many close with the outcome evidenced and the customer informed.\n\nA case is resolved when the money is found, not when the queue is cleared.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nSpecify closure as an evidence gate, not as a status change. A case may only close when the outcome is evidenced: the funds located, the return received, or the customer confirming receipt. If the evidence is not there, the case stays open, however old it gets, and the ageing is reported honestly rather than hidden by a closure.\n\nRequire these closure controls:\n\n→ An outcome code backed by evidence, not just a free text note\n→ A customer confirmation step before closure, wherever the customer raised the case\n→ A block on closure while a pacs.028 is still awaiting a pacs.029\n→ Ageing reported on open cases, so targets cannot be met by premature closure\n→ A reopen path that keeps the full history in one case, not a fresh start\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Can we close cases in three days?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves this case is resolved, and has the customer agreed?\"\n\nHave you ever seen a case closed to meet a target while the problem was still live?", "id": "WC-34", "level": "core", "module": "Core", "seq": 34, "source": "Public post", "takeaway": "A case is resolved when the money is found, not when the queue is cleared.", "title": "The case was closed as resolved, and the money was still missing", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Case opened", "note": "A missing payment of EUR 9,800 is reported and a case is opened."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "pacs.028 sent", "note": "The investigation request goes out to the beneficiary bank."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Outcome evidenced?", "note": "Closure needs proof the money is found, not just a message sent."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Closure blocked", "note": "The case stays open while the pacs.029 is still awaited."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Customer confirmation", "note": "The customer confirms receipt before the case can close."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Honest ageing", "note": "Ageing is reported on open cases, never hidden by a closure."}]}}, "week": 34, "agenda": "Work through this case: The case was closed as resolved, and the money was still missing. A customer reported a missing payment of EUR 9,800. An investigation case was opened, and a pacs.028 was sent. Three days later, the case was marked resolved. The customer had not been paid, had not been contacted, and had not agreed to anything. The money was still missing. The queue simply looked better. One thing to remember: A case is resolved when the money is found, not when the queue is cleared.", "thumbnail": "assets/thumbs/case-WC-34.svg"}, {"explain": "A customer sent a payment from a branch at 3.40 in the afternoon.\nThe branch officer said it had been sent.\nThe customer called the contact centre an hour later and was told it was queued.\nThe customer called the branch again and was told, again, that it had been sent.\nThe payment went out the next morning, after the cut-off had passed.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ The payment was accepted at the branch after the cut-off for same day processing\n→ The branch system showed the instruction as sent, meaning sent to operations\n→ The operations queue held it for the next processing window\n→ The contact centre read the operations status, and said queued\n→ The customer heard two different answers from the same bank\n→ The beneficiary expected the funds that day, and they did not arrive\n→ The payment settled the next morning, a day later than the customer expected\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Everyone in the bank means the same thing by sent.\"\n\nThey did not. To the branch, sent meant the instruction had left the branch. To operations, sent meant the payment had left the bank. To the customer, sent meant the money was on its way to the beneficiary that day. Three definitions, one word, and the customer caught the difference.\n\nWhy?\n\nCut-off times are where this confusion becomes expensive. A payment accepted after cut-off is not late, it is simply a next day payment, but only if someone tells the customer that. The branch system, the operations queue and the contact centre each showed a true fact about a different stage of the journey, and none of them showed the fact the customer needed: when the beneficiary would receive the funds. The customer was not given wrong information. The customer was given partial information, twice, in words that sounded final.\n\nThe fix is a shared vocabulary with a single definition for each status, used identically in every channel, plus the one piece of information that matters most: the expected value date. A customer who is told at the branch that the payment will be processed the next working day can plan around it. A customer who is told it has been sent, and then told it is queued, cannot plan around anything, and will call again. Every status word in a payment system should answer the question the customer is actually asking, which is not where the instruction is, but when the money will arrive.\n\nSent must mean one thing, everywhere, or it means nothing to the customer.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nWhen you specify payment status, specify the vocabulary before the screens. Each status needs one definition, one owner, and one meaning in every channel, and the definitions must be tested with the people who will explain them to customers. A status that means different things in different systems is not a status, it is a misunderstanding waiting for a cut-off.\n\nInsist on these:\n\n→ One status dictionary, shared by branch, operations and contact centre\n→ A displayed value date alongside every status, so the customer sees when funds arrive\n→ A cut-off warning at the point of instruction, not after it\n→ Queue visibility for the contact centre, so it never contradicts the branch\n→ A test that walks one payment through every channel and compares the words used\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Can each system show its own status?\"\n\nIt is \"Does every channel tell the customer the same thing, in words the customer understands?\"\n\nHave you ever heard two teams describe the same payment with opposite words, and both were right?", "id": "WC-35", "level": "core", "module": "Core", "seq": 35, "source": "Public post", "takeaway": "Sent must mean one thing, everywhere, or it means nothing to the customer.", "title": "The branch said sent, operations said queued, and the customer heard both", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "One payment, three meanings of sent", "parts": [{"label": "Branch", "note": "Sent meant the instruction had left the branch"}, {"label": "Operations queue", "note": "Queued meant held for the next processing window"}, {"label": "Contact centre", "note": "The team that read the operations status and contradicted the branch"}, {"label": "Cut-off", "note": "The line the payment had already passed when it was accepted"}, {"label": "Value date", "note": "The fact the customer needed: when the beneficiary would receive the funds"}, {"label": "Shared vocabulary", "note": "One definition per status, used identically in every channel"}]}}, "week": 35, "agenda": "Work through this case: The branch said sent, operations said queued, and the customer heard both. A customer sent a payment from a branch at 3.40 in the afternoon. The branch officer said it had been sent. The customer called the contact centre an hour later and was told it was queued. The customer called the branch again and was told, again, that it had been sent. The payment went out the next morning, after the. One thing to remember: Sent must mean one thing, everywhere, or it means nothing to the customer.", "thumbnail": "assets/thumbs/case-WC-35.svg"}, {"explain": "On a Monday morning, the repair queue held 400 items.\nTwo analysts were available to work it.\nThe SLA for the oldest items expired on Friday.\nSome items were worth millions. Some were worth very little.\nEvery item could not be fixed in time, so the team had to choose what would break.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ 400 payments sat in the repair queue, held for missing or incorrect data\n→ Two analysts could each clear around 40 items a day\n→ The queue was worked oldest first, as it always had been\n→ High value items with same day value dates waited behind low value items\n→ By Wednesday, the oldest items were clearing, but the urgent ones were ageing\n→ On Friday, the SLA was breached on the items that mattered most\n→ The queue was smaller, and the damage was larger\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"First in, first out is the fair way to work a queue.\"\n\nFirst in, first out is fair to the queue. It is not fair to the customer whose payment is time critical, or to the bank that will pay the cost of a missed value date. Fairness to items and fairness to outcomes are different things, and a queue worked by arrival order optimises for the wrong one.\n\nWhy?\n\nTriage is a decision about what breaks, made under pressure, with incomplete information. The factors that should drive it are visible in the payment itself: the value date, the amount, the customer, the reason for repair, and how close the item is to breaching. An item held for a missing character in an address, due to settle today, is not the same as an item held for a sanctions check that cannot be rushed, due next week. Working them in arrival order treats them as equal, and equality in a queue is how the urgent item becomes the breached item.\n\nThe dilemma is that triage rules feel like favouritism. Skipping an old, low value item to fix a new, high value one looks like the queue is being gamed, and the analyst who does it needs a rule to point to, not just judgement. That is why triage must be specified, published and measured: the rule decides, the analyst applies it, and the exceptions are recorded. Without a rule, triage happens anyway, informally, inconsistently, and invisibly, and the team cannot defend its choices when the breach is reviewed. A queue of 400 with two people is not a staffing problem alone. It is a prioritisation problem, and prioritisation without rules is just hope.\n\nTriage is a decision about what breaks first, so it must be a rule, not a habit.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nSpecify the repair queue as a prioritised worklist, not as a list. The priority must be calculated from the payment data, visible to the analyst, and explainable to anyone who reviews it. The queue should also show what will breach, and when, so the team works on the future breach, not just the oldest item.\n\nBuild the queue around these:\n\n→ A priority score from value date, amount, customer impact and time to breach\n→ A published triage rule the team can point to, not informal judgement\n→ A breach forecast that shows which items will fail, and when\n→ Automation for the most common repair reasons, so people work the exceptions\n→ A record of every triage decision, so choices can be defended later\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Can the team work the queue in order?\"\n\nIt is \"When not everything can be fixed, what breaks first, and who decided?\"\n\nHave you ever watched a queue get smaller while the most important items got older?", "id": "WC-36", "level": "advanced", "module": "Advanced", "seq": 36, "source": "Public post", "takeaway": "Triage is a decision about what breaks first, so it must be a rule, not a habit.", "title": "Four hundred repairs, two people, and an SLA breach by Friday", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Queue assessed", "note": "400 items, two analysts, and a Friday deadline that cannot all be met."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Priority scored", "note": "Value date, amount, customer impact and time to breach set the order."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Triage rule applied", "note": "The published rule decides what breaks first, not habit or arrival order."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Urgent items fixed", "note": "Time critical payments are repaired before they breach."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Breach forecast", "note": "The queue shows what will fail, and when, before it happens."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Automation", "note": "Common repair reasons are automated, so people work the exceptions."}]}}, "week": 36, "agenda": "Work through this case: Four hundred repairs, two people, and an SLA breach by Friday. On a Monday morning, the repair queue held 400 items. Two analysts were available to work it. The SLA for the oldest items expired on Friday. Some items were worth millions. Some were worth very little. Every item could not be fixed in time, so the team had to choose what would break. One thing to remember: Triage is a decision about what breaks first, so it must be a rule, not a habit.", "thumbnail": "assets/thumbs/case-WC-36.svg"}, {"explain": "A customer checked the status of a EUR 60,000 payment.\nThe screen said pending.\nThe customer waited, called, and was told it was still pending.\nThe beneficiary confirmed the funds had arrived two days earlier.\nThe payment had settled. Only the status had not.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ pacs.008 was sent, and the payment settled on the value date\n→ The core ledger recorded the settlement correctly\n→ The payment hub held a different status, because of a mapping error\n→ The mapping table translated the settled code to pending\n→ The customer screen read the hub status, and showed pending\n→ The customer nearly sent the payment a second time\n→ The error was found only when the beneficiary confirmed receipt\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"If two systems hold the same payment, they hold the same status.\"\n\nThey hold the same payment identifier, and that is all they are guaranteed to share. The status in each system is produced by its own logic, its own mapping table and its own update timing, and any of those can diverge without either system reporting an error.\n\nWhy?\n\nStatus mapping is one of the quietest failure points in a payment architecture. A code that means settled in one system can mean something else entirely in another, and the translation between them lives in a mapping table that is rarely tested as thoroughly as the payment flow itself. When the mapping is wrong, nothing fails loudly. The payment settles, the ledger balances, and the customer is shown a status that is simply untrue. The failure is invisible to every control that checks money movement, because the money moved correctly. Only the information about the money is wrong, and information is what the customer acts on.\n\nThe near duplicate in this case is the warning. A customer who believes a payment is pending will chase it, re-send it, or lose confidence in the bank's information entirely. The fix is to treat status as data that must be reconciled, just like money. The hub status and the ledger state must be compared continuously, and any disagreement must raise an exception before the customer sees it. A mapping table is code, and it needs the same testing, versioning and change control as any other code that touches a payment.\n\nStatus must come from settlement evidence, not from a mapping table.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nWhen you specify an integration between two payment systems, specify the status reconciliation, not just the message flow. Every status code in the source must have a tested mapping in the target, and every status the customer sees must be traceable to the evidence that produced it. A status with no evidence behind it is a guess with a label.\n\nRequire these in the integration:\n\n→ A tested mapping for every status code, including the codes that rarely occur\n→ Continuous reconciliation between hub status and ledger state\n→ An exception when the two disagree, raised before the customer sees either\n→ Change control on the mapping table, with a test for every change\n→ A customer status that says unknown, rather than a confident word the evidence does not support\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Can the hub show a status?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence supports this status, and what happens when two systems disagree?\"\n\nHave you ever seen a mapping table quietly rewrite the truth about a payment?", "id": "WC-37", "level": "advanced", "module": "Advanced", "seq": 37, "source": "Public post", "takeaway": "Status must come from settlement evidence, not from a mapping table.", "title": "The screen said pending, but the payment had already settled", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Settled in the ledger, pending on the screen", "parts": [{"label": "Core ledger", "note": "The record that correctly showed the payment as settled"}, {"label": "Payment hub", "note": "The system that held a different status for the same payment"}, {"label": "Mapping table", "note": "The translation that turned a settled code into pending"}, {"label": "Customer screen", "note": "The display that read the hub status and showed pending"}, {"label": "Reconciliation", "note": "The continuous check that should have caught the disagreement"}, {"label": "Unknown status", "note": "The honest word to show when the evidence does not support a confident one"}]}}, "week": 37, "agenda": "Work through this case: The screen said pending, but the payment had already settled. A customer checked the status of a EUR 60,000 payment. The screen said pending. The customer waited, called, and was told it was still pending. The beneficiary confirmed the funds had arrived two days earlier. The payment had settled. Only the status had not. One thing to remember: Status must come from settlement evidence, not from a mapping table.", "thumbnail": "assets/thumbs/case-WC-37.svg"}, {"explain": "At the end of the month, the nostro reconciliation showed a break of EUR 0.01.\nThe team noted it, and moved on. One cent is not worth chasing.\nThree weeks later, a EUR 100,000 payment was found to have settled to the wrong account.\nThe one cent break had hidden it.\nTwo errors had netted off, and the net looked like nothing.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ A payment of EUR 100,000 was settled to the wrong nostro account\n→ A second error, of EUR 99,999.99, went the other way\n→ The two errors netted to a break of EUR 0.01\n→ The reconciliation reported the net position, and the break looked trivial\n→ The break was below the investigation threshold, so no one looked\n→ The EUR 100,000 error sat unnoticed for three weeks\n→ It was found by chance, during an unrelated review\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"A small break means a small problem.\"\n\nA small net break means only that the errors nearly cancel out. It says nothing about the size of the errors underneath, and a net figure is the one number in reconciliation that can hide the largest mistakes.\n\nWhy?\n\nNetting is the enemy of reconciliation, because reconciliation exists to find individual errors, and netting combines them into a single number that conceals them. Two large errors in opposite directions produce a small net break, and a threshold that says investigate breaks above EUR 50 will wave both through. The gross position, the sum of every unmatched item regardless of direction, is the number that tells the truth about the health of the account. A gross break of EUR 199,999.99 with a net of EUR 0.01 is not a healthy account. It is an account with two serious errors and a reporting method that hid them.\n\nThe discipline that prevents this is simple to state and demanding to operate: every break is investigated on its own, regardless of size, and breaks are never netted for reporting or for ageing. A one cent break is investigated because it is a break, not because it is one cent. The cost is real, because it means more investigation work, and more breaks that turn out to be rounding or timing. The alternative is the case above: a six figure error, invisible for three weeks, found by luck. Reconciliation that only chases large net breaks is not a control. It is a filter that lets the most dangerous errors through.\n\nBreaks that net to nearly nothing can still hide a very large error.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nSpecify reconciliation on gross breaks, with no netting, no threshold that suppresses investigation, and ageing that starts the day the break appears. A break is evidence that two records disagree, and the size of the disagreement is not a measure of its importance. The smallest break in the report can be the symptom of the largest error in the book.\n\nInsist on these reconciliation rules:\n\n→ Gross reporting, with every unmatched item shown separately\n→ No netting of breaks, in reporting, ageing or investigation\n→ Investigation of every break, with rounding and timing proved, not assumed\n→ Ageing from the day the break appears, with escalation as it gets older\n→ A review of the breaks that were cleared, to catch the ones cleared too quickly\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Can we reconcile to a small net difference?\"\n\nIt is \"Can we prove that every individual item matches, with nothing hidden by netting?\"\n\nHave you ever seen a tiny break turn out to be the visible tip of a much larger error?", "id": "WC-38", "level": "advanced", "module": "Advanced", "seq": 38, "source": "Public post", "takeaway": "Breaks that net to nearly nothing can still hide a very large error.", "title": "A EUR 0.01 nostro break hid a EUR 100,000 error", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Break appears", "note": "The reconciliation shows a net break of EUR 0.01."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Gross or net?", "note": "The gross position of EUR 199,999.99 tells the true story."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Investigate each break", "note": "Every unmatched item is examined on its own, regardless of size."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Netting refused", "note": "Breaks are never combined for reporting, ageing or investigation."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Ageing and escalation", "note": "Ageing starts the day the break appears, with escalation as it grows older."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Error found", "note": "The EUR 100,000 error is found by the control, not by chance."}]}}, "week": 38, "agenda": "Work through this case: A EUR 0.01 nostro break hid a EUR 100,000 error. At the end of the month, the nostro reconciliation showed a break of EUR 0.01. The team noted it, and moved on. One cent is not worth chasing. Three weeks later, a EUR 100,000 payment was found to have settled to the wrong account. The one cent break had hidden it. Two errors had netted off, and the net looked like. One thing to remember: Breaks that net to nearly nothing can still hide a very large error.", "thumbnail": "assets/thumbs/case-WC-38.svg"}, {"explain": "A customer sent an instruction to the bank: pay urgently.\nThe instruction named the beneficiary and the amount, EUR 75,000.\nIt gave no value date and no definition of urgent.\nThe payment went out as a next day payment.\nThe customer had meant same day, and the supplier's deadline was missed.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ The customer instructed the payment with the words pay urgently\n→ No value date was specified in the instruction\n→ The operations team applied the standard next day processing\n→ The payment settled the following day\n→ The supplier deadline passed, and the customer faced a penalty\n→ The customer said the bank had been told it was urgent\n→ The bank said urgent was never defined\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Urgent means the same thing to everyone.\"\n\nIt does not. To the customer, urgent meant today. To the operations team, urgent was a word with no operational meaning, so the standard process applied. The instruction was followed as written, and the outcome was still a failure, because the word that mattered most had no definition.\n\nWhy?\n\nAmbiguity in a payment instruction is not a minor inconvenience. It is a gap that someone must fill, and whoever fills it takes on a decision that was never theirs to make. A same day guess may apply a cut-off, a fee or a channel the customer did not intend. A next day guess may miss the deadline the customer cared about. Either guess can be wrong, and the guesser will be blamed for the outcome either way. The only safe response to an ambiguous instruction is not to guess, but to confirm, and confirmation takes time that an urgent payment does not have. That is the trap: the more urgent the payment, the less time there is to resolve the ambiguity, and the more damage the ambiguity causes.\n\nThe answer belongs in the instruction itself. A payment instruction needs a value date, stated explicitly, and a channel or service that defines what happens by when. Where the customer uses words like urgent, the system must translate them into a value date at the point of instruction, and show the customer the translation before the payment is accepted. Urgent becomes same day, value date today, and the customer either confirms it or corrects it. The decision is then made by the person who knows, when it can still be changed, not by a team reading a word that could mean anything.\n\nUrgency is a value date, not a feeling. If it is not specified, it must be confirmed.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nNever let a free text instruction carry a time critical decision. Specify the value date as a mandatory field, with a default that is shown to the customer and can be challenged, and a confirmation step that states the value date in plain words before the payment is accepted. Words like urgent and immediate must be translated into dates at entry, or rejected as instructions.\n\nBuild these into the instruction flow:\n\n→ A mandatory value date on every payment instruction\n→ A translation of urgency words into a value date, shown to the customer\n→ A confirmation step that states when the beneficiary will receive the funds\n→ A rule that ambiguous instructions are confirmed, never guessed\n→ A record of the confirmed value date, so the decision is evidenced\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Can we accept a payment instruction quickly?\"\n\nIt is \"Does the customer know, before we accept it, exactly when the money will arrive?\"\n\nHave you ever had to guess what a customer meant by urgent, and guessed wrong?", "id": "WC-39", "level": "core", "module": "Core", "seq": 39, "source": "Public post", "takeaway": "Urgency is a value date, not a feeling. If it is not specified, it must be confirmed.", "title": "Pay urgently, with no value date: who decides what urgent means", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Pay urgently, no value date, missed deadline", "parts": [{"label": "Instruction", "note": "The words pay urgently, with a beneficiary and an amount but no date"}, {"label": "Value date", "note": "The mandatory field that should have carried the decision"}, {"label": "Cut-off", "note": "The line that decides whether today is still possible"}, {"label": "Confirmation", "note": "The step that states when the beneficiary will receive the funds"}, {"label": "Translation of urgent", "note": "The conversion of a word into a date, shown before acceptance"}, {"label": "Evidence", "note": "The record of the confirmed value date, so the decision can be proved"}]}}, "week": 39, "agenda": "Work through this case: Pay urgently, with no value date: who decides what urgent means. A customer sent an instruction to the bank: pay urgently. The instruction named the beneficiary and the amount, EUR 75,000. It gave no value date and no definition of urgent. The payment went out as a next day payment. The customer had meant same day, and the supplier's deadline was missed. One thing to remember: Urgency is a value date, not a feeling. If it is not specified, it must be confirmed.", "thumbnail": "assets/thumbs/case-WC-39.svg"}, {"explain": "A guest checked out of a hotel on a Friday morning.\nThe card payment of EUR 1,850 had been approved four days earlier.\nThe hotel never sent the clearing record.\nThe money stayed in limbo for three weeks, and the customer ended up paying twice.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ Card presented at check-in, EUR 1,850 authorisation requested\n→ Issuer approves, hold placed on the available balance\n→ Guest checks out, expecting the hold to become the final charge\n→ Acquirer batch fails, clearing record never sent\n→ Hold expires, available balance restored in full\n→ Customer pays the hotel again by bank transfer, believing the card payment failed\n→ Late clearing arrives on day 21, account debited a second time\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Show the authorisation as pending until clearing arrives, then show the final charge.\"\n\nSo the app showed pending for a week, then showed nothing at all. The customer reasonably concluded the card payment had failed. Nothing on the screen said a late clearing could still arrive, or that the hold expiring was not the same as the payment being cancelled.\n\nWhy?\n\nAuthorisation and clearing are two separate legs with separate clocks. Authorisation asks one question, is this card good for this amount right now, and it answers in seconds. Clearing is the merchant claiming the money, and it travels in batches, hours or days later, under scheme time limits that can stretch to weeks. The hold is the bridge between them, and the hold has an expiry date. When the hold expires without clearing, the issuer must release the funds, because holding a customer's money forever on a promise that may never arrive is not a defensible rule. But releasing the hold does not cancel the merchant's right to present. A late presentment is still a valid claim, and it lands on an account that has already moved on.\n\nThe shadow posting is not a system error. It is what happens when two correct rules, the hold must expire and the merchant may present late, meet in one account. The customer sees one payment. The system sees two events that never shook hands, and the reconciliation team sees an authorisation with no clearing record, ageing in a queue nobody owns.\n\nAn expired hold is not a cancelled payment, and an approved authorisation is not money moved.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nDesign for the gap between authorisation and clearing, not just for the happy path where clearing follows in hours. The customer needs to see what is true at each stage, and the operations team needs a queue for authorisations that never clear.\n\n→ Authorisation, permission granted, funds ring-fenced, nothing moved yet\n→ Clearing, the merchant's claim, arriving in batches on its own timetable\n→ Hold expiry, the ring-fence released, not the payment cancelled\n→ Late presentment, a valid claim landing on a settled account\n→ Reconciliation, matching each clearing record to its authorisation, and ageing the orphans\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we release the hold?\"\n\nIt is \"What does the customer see, and what can operations do, when clearing is late or never arrives?\"\n\nHave you ever watched a pending card charge vanish, only for the money to leave weeks later?", "id": "WC-40", "level": "core", "module": "Core", "seq": 40, "source": "Public post", "takeaway": "An expired hold is not a cancelled payment, and an approved authorisation is not money moved.", "title": "EUR 1,850 approved at the hotel, and the clearing never arrived", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "EUR 1,850 approved, clearing never arrived", "parts": [{"label": "Authorisation", "note": "Permission in seconds, funds ring-fenced, nothing moved yet"}, {"label": "Hold", "note": "The bridge between authorisation and clearing, with an expiry date"}, {"label": "Clearing", "note": "The merchant's claim, travelling in batches on its own timetable"}, {"label": "Hold expiry", "note": "Funds released to the customer, payment not cancelled"}, {"label": "Late presentment", "note": "A valid claim landing on an account that has moved on"}, {"label": "Reconciliation", "note": "Match every clearing record to its authorisation, age the orphans"}]}}, "week": 40, "agenda": "Work through this case: EUR 1,850 approved at the hotel, and the clearing never arrived. A guest checked out of a hotel on a Friday morning. The card payment of EUR 1,850 had been approved four days earlier. The hotel never sent the clearing record. The money stayed in limbo for three weeks, and the customer ended up paying twice. One thing to remember: An expired hold is not a cancelled payment, and an approved authorisation is not money moved.", "thumbnail": "assets/thumbs/case-WC-40.svg"}, {"explain": "A customer reported a EUR 640 card payment as fraud.\nThe merchant produced a delivery photo, a signature, and a matching delivery address.\nThe issuer had to decide who to believe, and the evidence pointed both ways.\nWhatever the decision, one honest party would feel betrayed.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ Order placed online, card charged EUR 640, goods shipped the same day\n→ Parcel delivered, photo taken, signature captured at the door\n→ Customer reports the transaction as fraud, issuer credits the account provisionally\n→ Merchant disputes the chargeback with delivery evidence, representment filed\n→ Issuer compares device, address and purchase history with the fraud claim\n→ Decision due, and either side can escalate to pre-arbitration if it disagrees\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The customer is always right, so a fraud claim always wins.\"\n\nSo the first instinct was to side with the customer and close the case. But the merchant's evidence was real, and the delivery address matched the cardholder's own address. Siding with the customer here would punish an honest merchant and teach every fraudster that a claim beats proof. Siding with the merchant would leave a genuine victim unprotected if the claim was true.\n\nWhy?\n\nChargebacks run on reason codes and evidence, not on sympathy. A fraud reason code says the cardholder did not authorise the transaction, and the issuer must test that claim against what the merchant can prove. Delivery to the cardholder's own address, a signature, and a device that matches previous genuine orders all weaken a fraud claim, while a new device, a new address, and a first purchase of high value goods strengthen it. The uncomfortable middle is friendly fraud, where the cardholder did receive the goods and disputes the charge anyway. Friendly fraud looks identical to true fraud in the first alert, and only the evidence separates them.\n\nThe issuer also weighs the liability shift. When strong authentication was used, the liability for a fraud claim can sit with the issuer rather than the merchant, which changes who pays but not who is telling the truth. Provisional credit protects the customer while the case is investigated, but it is not a verdict. A verdict needs the full evidence pack, and it needs a decision maker who is willing to decline a fraud claim when the proof says the goods arrived.\n\nIn a chargeback, evidence decides, not the loudest claim.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nBuild the dispute flow around the evidence pack, not around the speed of the refund. Every party deserves a decision it can understand, even when it loses.\n\n→ Fraud reason code, what the cardholder claims, tested, never assumed\n→ Representment, the merchant's evidence pack, delivery, signature, device\n→ Liability shift, who pays when strong authentication was used\n→ Provisional credit, protection during investigation, not a final decision\n→ Pre-arbitration, the escalation when issuer and acquirer disagree\n\nThe real requirement is not \"How fast can we refund the customer?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence pack decides a disputed claim fairly, and who is accountable for the decision?\"\n\nHave you ever had to tell a customer that the evidence said the goods were delivered?", "id": "WC-41", "level": "core", "module": "Core", "seq": 41, "source": "Public post", "takeaway": "In a chargeback, evidence decides, not the loudest claim.", "title": "EUR 640 headphones, delivery proof, and a fraud claim", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Order placed", "note": "Card charged EUR 640, goods shipped the same day."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Delivery proof", "note": "Photo and signature captured at the cardholder's address."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Fraud claim", "note": "Customer disputes the charge, provisional credit applied."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Representment", "note": "Merchant files delivery evidence against the chargeback."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Evidence decision", "note": "Device, address and history tested against the claim."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Pre-arbitration", "note": "Escalation route when issuer and acquirer disagree."}]}}, "week": 41, "agenda": "Work through this case: EUR 640 headphones, delivery proof, and a fraud claim. A customer reported a EUR 640 card payment as fraud. The merchant produced a delivery photo, a signature, and a matching delivery address. The issuer had to decide who to believe, and the evidence pointed both ways. Whatever the decision, one honest party would feel betrayed. One thing to remember: In a chargeback, evidence decides, not the loudest claim.", "thumbnail": "assets/thumbs/case-WC-41.svg"}, {"explain": "A customer tried to book flights worth EUR 2,400.\nThe security check failed twice, and the booking was abandoned.\nThe customer booked with a competitor within the hour.\nThe fraud that the check prevented never existed.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ Customer starts checkout on a new phone, travelling abroad\n→ Risk engine scores the transaction high, new device, unusual location, high value\n→ Challenge flow triggered, authentication through the banking app required\n→ App notification never arrives, mobile signal is weak, code expires twice\n→ Customer abandons the purchase and the merchant loses the sale\n→ Competitor checkout passes with a frictionless check, booking completed\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Every high value transaction must be challenged, whatever the cost.\"\n\nSo the rule challenged every transaction over a fixed threshold. It caught fraud, and it also caught a genuine customer at the worst possible moment, abroad, on a new device, with a deadline. The fraud team celebrated a blocked transaction. Nobody measured the sale that never happened, because no alert fires for revenue that walks away quietly.\n\nWhy?\n\nStrong customer authentication is a legal duty, but the law allows risk based exemptions, and the art is in using them. A low value payment, a trusted beneficiary the customer has paid before, and a transaction the risk engine scores as low can all pass without a challenge, under transaction risk analysis. The challenge flow exists for the transactions that genuinely look risky, and it must work when it is triggered, on the device the customer actually has, with a fallback that does not depend on one signal or one network.\n\nThe cost of a false decline is invisible in fraud reports, because no fraud is reported, no alert fires, and no case is opened. The customer simply leaves, and the revenue goes to a competitor whose checks were better tuned. Security teams measure fraud prevented. Nobody owns the revenue lost to friction, so friction grows until a customer complains loudly enough. The right question is not how much fraud the challenge stopped, but how much genuine business it stopped alongside the fraud, and whether the challenge itself worked for the people it was meant to protect.\n\nA security check that blocks good customers is not secure, it is expensive.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nTune authentication by risk, not by threshold alone, and measure what friction costs with the same rigour as what fraud costs.\n\n→ Frictionless flow, low risk passes without a challenge\n→ Challenge flow, step up only when the risk genuinely requires it\n→ Exemptions, low value, trusted beneficiary, transaction risk analysis\n→ Fallbacks, a second authentication path when the first fails\n→ False declines, measured as lost revenue, not ignored as no fraud\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Challenge every risky transaction?\"\n\nIt is \"Which transactions genuinely need a challenge, and what happens when the challenge itself fails?\"\n\nHave you ever abandoned a purchase because the security check would not let you through?", "id": "WC-42", "level": "core", "module": "Core", "seq": 42, "source": "Public post", "takeaway": "A security check that blocks good customers is not secure, it is expensive.", "title": "A EUR 2,400 flight booking lost to a failed security check", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Checkout started", "note": "New phone, abroad, EUR 2,400 flights in the basket."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Risk high?", "note": "New device, unusual location and high value raise the score."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Challenge flow", "note": "Authentication required through the banking app."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Authentication fails", "note": "Weak signal, code expires twice, no fallback offered."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Abandonment", "note": "Customer leaves, merchant loses a genuine sale."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Competitor converts", "note": "Frictionless check passes, booking completed within the hour."}]}}, "week": 42, "agenda": "Work through this case: A EUR 2,400 flight booking lost to a failed security check. A customer tried to book flights worth EUR 2,400. The security check failed twice, and the booking was abandoned. The customer booked with a competitor within the hour. The fraud that the check prevented never existed. One thing to remember: A security check that blocks good customers is not secure, it is expensive.", "thumbnail": "assets/thumbs/case-WC-42.svg"}, {"explain": "A small charity sent EUR 4,500 every month to field partners.\nTransaction monitoring flagged every payment.\nThe bank closed the account, and the charity started moving cash.\nThe monitoring system had made the money less visible, not more.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ Charity opens account, declares its purpose and its field partners\n→ Monthly payments to a high risk jurisdiction, similar amounts, similar dates\n→ Monitoring rules fire, pattern matches structuring and layering typologies\n→ Analysts review, alerts closed as expected activity, then reopened by the next rule change\n→ Relationship team decides the account costs more to monitor than it earns\n→ Account exited, charity moves to cash couriers and informal channels\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Exiting risky customers removes the risk.\"\n\nSo the bank exited the whole category, one charity at a time. Each exit removed one file from the monitoring queue. None of the exits removed the underlying activity. The money still moved, through channels no bank could see, and no report could reach. The queue looked healthier. The financial system was blinder.\n\nWhy?\n\nTransaction monitoring is built on rules and typologies, and a charity paying field partners in a high risk jurisdiction matches several of them at once: regular amounts, cross border, cash intensive destination. The rules cannot tell a genuine aid payment from a layered transfer, because the pattern is the same and only the context differs. That context lives in the customer file, in the purpose declared at onboarding, in the field reports, and in the relationship manager's knowledge, and none of it reaches the monitoring engine.\n\nThe risk based approach requires the bank to understand its customer and manage the risk proportionately, not to exit every customer whose pattern looks difficult. De-risking feels safe because the alert queue shrinks, but it pushes legitimate activity into informal channels where there is no monitoring at all, no reporting, and no trail. The regulator does not ask whether the bank avoided risk. The regulator asks whether the bank understood it, documented it, and reported what was genuinely suspicious. A suspicious activity report is a judgement, not a rule output, and closing an account is not a substitute for making one.\n\nExiting a customer does not remove the risk, it removes the visibility.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nConnect monitoring to customer context, so the engine judges behaviour against expected activity instead of judging patterns in isolation.\n\n→ Typologies, patterns the rules match, never proof on their own\n→ Customer context, purpose and expected activity, fed into monitoring\n→ Risk based approach, manage proportionately, do not exit by category\n→ Suspicious activity report, a human judgement, filed on suspicion, not on volume\n→ Financial inclusion, the cost of an exit measured in lost visibility\n\nThe real requirement is not \"How do we clear the alert queue?\"\n\nIt is \"How does monitoring use customer context, so genuine activity is understood and only genuine suspicion is reported?\"\n\nHave you ever seen a control that made the risk harder to see?", "id": "WC-43", "level": "advanced", "module": "Advanced", "seq": 43, "source": "Public post", "takeaway": "Exiting a customer does not remove the risk, it removes the visibility.", "title": "The charity whose payments looked like money laundering", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Charity payments flagged, account exited", "parts": [{"label": "Typologies", "note": "Patterns the rules match, never proof on their own"}, {"label": "Customer context", "note": "Purpose and expected activity, missing from the engine"}, {"label": "Risk based approach", "note": "Manage proportionately, do not exit by category"}, {"label": "Suspicious report", "note": "A human judgement, filed on suspicion, not on volume"}, {"label": "De-risking", "note": "Exits shrink the queue and push flows underground"}, {"label": "Financial inclusion", "note": "The cost of an exit, measured in lost visibility"}]}}, "week": 43, "agenda": "Work through this case: The charity whose payments looked like money laundering. A small charity sent EUR 4,500 every month to field partners. Transaction monitoring flagged every payment. The bank closed the account, and the charity started moving cash. The monitoring system had made the money less visible, not more. One thing to remember: Exiting a customer does not remove the risk, it removes the visibility.", "thumbnail": "assets/thumbs/case-WC-43.svg"}, {"explain": "A payment file with 380 supplier account numbers was emailed to the wrong address.\nThe addresses differed by one letter.\nThe email could not be recalled, and the file could not be unseen.\nThe breach clock had already started.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ Supplier payment file prepared, names, account numbers, amounts, EUR 210,000 total\n→ File attached to an email, addressed from memory, one letter wrong\n→ Email delivered to an external inbox outside the bank's control\n→ Recipient opens the file before the sender notices the error\n→ Recall attempted, too late, the message is already read\n→ Incident declared, beneficiaries warned, regulator notified within the deadline\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Email is fine for payment files, because we have always done it this way.\"\n\nSo the file travelled by the least secure channel in the building, with no encryption, no access control, and no confirmation of the address. The control that should have caught it was a human reading an address carefully, at the end of a long day, under deadline pressure. That is not a control. That is a hope, and hope fails exactly when the pressure is highest.\n\nWhy?\n\nAccount numbers combined with names and amounts are enough to attempt payment fraud, from fake supplier invoices to social engineering calls that sound convincing because the caller knows real details. The damage is not the email itself, it is the loss of control over data that cannot be taken back. Data protection law treats this as a personal data breach, and the notification clock runs from discovery, typically 72 hours to the regulator, with affected people told without undue delay when the risk to them is high. Hoping the recipient deletes the file is not a response, and delaying disclosure to avoid embarrassment makes the regulatory position worse.\n\nThe real failure happened before the email was sent, in a process that allowed a payment file to leave through email at all. Secure file transfer, encryption, address confirmation, and data loss prevention rules that block account numbers in outbound mail are all standard controls, and none of them were in place. A payment file is a payment instruction in waiting, and it deserves the same protection as the payment itself. The team that fixes the process after the breach is the team that should have fixed it before the breach.\n\nData that has been seen cannot be unseen, so the control must sit before the send button.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nRemove the unsafe path entirely, rather than adding warnings to it. People under pressure will always take the path that works, so make the safe path the easy path.\n\n→ Secure transfer, payment files move through controlled channels, never email\n→ Encryption, files unreadable to anyone but the intended recipient\n→ Address confirmation, validated addresses, never typed from memory\n→ Data loss prevention, outbound rules that block account numbers in mail\n→ Breach response, clock, notifications, and warnings rehearsed before they are needed\n\nThe real requirement is not \"How do we recall the email?\"\n\nIt is \"How do we make it impossible for a payment file to leave through an uncontrolled channel?\"\n\nHave you ever sent a file to the wrong person and realised there was no undo?", "id": "WC-44", "level": "core", "module": "Core", "seq": 44, "source": "Public post", "takeaway": "Data that has been seen cannot be unseen, so the control must sit before the send button.", "title": "A payment file with 380 account numbers went to the wrong inbox", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "File prepared", "note": "380 supplier account numbers, EUR 210,000 total."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Email sent", "note": "Address typed from memory, one letter wrong."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Wrong inbox", "note": "External recipient opens the file before anyone notices."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Recall in time?", "note": "Recall fails, the message is already read."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Breach declared", "note": "Incident process starts, the notification clock runs."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Notifications", "note": "Beneficiaries warned, regulator notified in time."}]}}, "week": 44, "agenda": "Work through this case: A payment file with 380 account numbers went to the wrong inbox. A payment file with 380 supplier account numbers was emailed to the wrong address. The addresses differed by one letter. The email could not be recalled, and the file could not be unseen. The breach clock had already started. One thing to remember: Data that has been seen cannot be unseen, so the control must sit before the send button.", "thumbnail": "assets/thumbs/case-WC-44.svg"}, {"explain": "A bank held back EUR 40 million of customer payments until late afternoon.\nThe payments were valid, funded, and ready to go.\nThe delay protected the bank's liquidity buffer, and the customers paid the price.\nOne corporate missed a settlement window it could not recover.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ Morning, treasury reviews the liquidity coverage ratio and the intraday position\n→ Buffer is thin, outflows are heavy, the ratio is close to its internal floor\n→ Operations instructed to queue non urgent payments until inflows arrive\n→ Corporate payments sit in the queue, customers see no status, no explanation\n→ Inflows arrive late afternoon, queue released, payments go out hours late\n→ One beneficiary misses its own settlement deadline, costs passed back to the sender\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Protect the liquidity buffer at all times, even if payments wait.\"\n\nSo the queue became the liquidity tool. It worked, in the narrow sense that the ratio held. It also meant the bank used its customers' payments as its buffer, without telling them, and without any rule for which payments could wait and which could not. Urgent and routine payments waited together, in the same silent queue.\n\nWhy?\n\nThe liquidity coverage ratio requires a bank to hold enough high quality liquid assets to survive 30 days of stress, and intraday liquidity is the separate discipline of having funds in the right settlement account at the right moment. Both are real duties, and a bank that ignores them puts itself and the system at risk. But delaying customer payments to protect a ratio creates a second risk that the ratio does not measure: gridlock. When one bank holds back its outflows, the receiving banks wait for inflows that do not arrive, and they hold back their own payments in turn. The delay propagates, and the system slows for everyone, including the bank that started it.\n\nPayment queues in a real time gross settlement system are designed to manage this, with priorities, bilateral limits, and central bank intraday credit, but those tools only help when operations uses them transparently, with rules for urgency, customer impact, and communication. A silent queue is a decision made without accountability. The customer whose payment waited had no chance to choose a different route, a different time, or a different bank, because nobody told them the payment was waiting at all.\n\nA payment delayed to protect a ratio is still a payment delayed, and the customer feels it first.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nMake queuing a governed decision with rules, owners, and customer communication, not a quiet instruction from treasury to operations.\n\n→ Liquidity coverage ratio, the 30 day buffer, a prudential duty, not a payment rule\n→ Intraday liquidity, funds in the right account at the right moment\n→ Payment queues, priorities and limits, used transparently, with customer impact rules\n→ Gridlock, one bank's delay becoming every bank's delay\n→ Communication, a held payment the customer knows about is a different problem from one they do not\n\nThe real requirement is not \"How do we protect the ratio?\"\n\nIt is \"Which payments may wait, for how long, under whose authority, and what do we tell the customer while they wait?\"\n\nHave you ever waited for a payment that was ready to go, with no explanation at all?", "id": "WC-45", "level": "advanced", "module": "Advanced", "seq": 45, "source": "Public post", "takeaway": "A payment delayed to protect a ratio is still a payment delayed, and the customer feels it first.", "title": "The bank that held EUR 40 million to protect its liquidity ratio", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "EUR 40 million queued to protect the buffer", "parts": [{"label": "Liquidity coverage ratio", "note": "The 30 day buffer, a prudential duty, not a payment rule"}, {"label": "Intraday liquidity", "note": "Funds in the right settlement account at the right moment"}, {"label": "Payment queue", "note": "Priorities and limits, needing transparency and rules"}, {"label": "Gridlock", "note": "One bank's delay becoming every bank's delay"}, {"label": "Customer impact", "note": "A missed settlement window the sender cannot recover"}, {"label": "Communication", "note": "A held payment explained is a different problem from a silent one"}]}}, "week": 45, "agenda": "Work through this case: The bank that held EUR 40 million to protect its liquidity ratio. A bank held back EUR 40 million of customer payments until late afternoon. The payments were valid, funded, and ready to go. The delay protected the bank's liquidity buffer, and the customers paid the price. One corporate missed a settlement window it could not recover. One thing to remember: A payment delayed to protect a ratio is still a payment delayed, and the customer feels it first.", "thumbnail": "assets/thumbs/case-WC-45.svg"}, {"explain": "At 3.07am on a Sunday, an account was emptied in 14 instant payments.\nNo member of staff was on duty.\nThe automated limits allowed every one of them.\nBy Monday morning, the money was gone through a chain of mule accounts.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ Account takeover, credentials stolen days earlier, fraudster waits for the quiet hours\n→ First payment, EUR 700, passes, below the per transaction limit\n→ Velocity limit set high to avoid blocking genuine weekend trade\n→ Fourteen payments in 22 minutes, each below the limit, each instant and irrevocable\n→ Funds split across mule accounts, withdrawn or forwarded within the hour\n→ Customer wakes, reports fraud, recall requests sent, most funds unrecoverable\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"A per transaction limit is enough to control instant payment fraud.\"\n\nSo the limit was set once, reviewed annually, and tuned for the busiest genuine trading day. It never considered time of day, device change, new payee, or the speed of 14 payments in 22 minutes. The fraudster understood the limit better than the bank did, and stayed just under it, payment after payment, while the system watched and approved.\n\nWhy?\n\nInstant payments settle in seconds, around the clock, and they cannot be recalled by the sender once settled. A recall request asks the beneficiary bank to return the funds, and it only works when the money is still there and the beneficiary bank can freeze it or the beneficiary agrees. Against mule accounts that empty within minutes, recall is a hope, not a control. Prevention is the only control that matters, and prevention in instant payments must be behavioural, not static.\n\nA payment at 3am from a new device to a new payee, followed by 13 more in 22 minutes, is a pattern no genuine customer produces, and a velocity control that counts payments per hour, per payee, and per device would have stopped it at the third or fourth. Payee verification, checking that the account name matches the account number before the payment is sent, adds a further check that catches misdirection and some mule setups. The dilemma is real: tight limits block genuine customers, especially small businesses trading at weekends, and every blocked payment is a complaint. But a limit that never blocks a genuine payment will never block a fraudster either, because the fraudster tests the limit and stays under it.\n\nIn instant payments, prevention is the only control, because recovery is a request, not a right.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nDesign controls for the pattern, not the single payment, and make them work at 3am with the same force as at 3pm.\n\n→ Velocity controls, counts per hour, per payee, per device, not one static limit\n→ Behavioural signals, time, device, new payee, combined into a risk score\n→ Payee verification, name matching before the payment leaves\n→ Recall, a request to the beneficiary bank, effective only while funds remain\n→ 24/7 monitoring, fraud does not keep office hours, so controls cannot either\n\nThe real requirement is not \"What should the limit be?\"\n\nIt is \"What pattern of payments should stop the flow in real time, at any hour, before the money leaves?\"\n\nHave you ever trusted a limit that a fraudster understood better than you did?", "id": "WC-46", "level": "advanced", "module": "Advanced", "seq": 46, "source": "Public post", "takeaway": "In instant payments, prevention is the only control, because recovery is a request, not a right.", "title": "EUR 9,800 stolen at 3am on a Sunday, in 14 instant payments", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Account takeover", "note": "Stolen credentials, fraudster waits for the quiet hours."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "First payment passes", "note": "EUR 700, below the per transaction limit."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Velocity breached?", "note": "High static limit never fires, pattern never scored."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Fourteen payments", "note": "22 minutes, each instant and irrevocable."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Mule accounts", "note": "Funds split and forwarded within the hour."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Recall requested", "note": "Most funds already gone, recovery unlikely."}]}}, "week": 46, "agenda": "Work through this case: EUR 9,800 stolen at 3am on a Sunday, in 14 instant payments. At 3.07am on a Sunday, an account was emptied in 14 instant payments. No member of staff was on duty. The automated limits allowed every one of them. By Monday morning, the money was gone through a chain of mule accounts. One thing to remember: In instant payments, prevention is the only control, because recovery is a request, not a right.", "thumbnail": "assets/thumbs/case-WC-46.svg"}, {"explain": "A relief payment of EUR 300 arrived with an expiry date.\nSpend it in 30 days, or lose it.\nThe recipient saved EUR 120 toward rent, and watched it disappear.\nThe money had done exactly what it was programmed to do.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ Pilot scheme issues relief funds as programmable central bank digital currency\n→ Payment carries conditions, spend within 30 days, only at approved merchants\n→ Recipient spends EUR 180 on essentials, keeps EUR 120 for the rent date\n→ Day 31, unspent balance expires and returns to the issuer automatically\n→ Recipient complains, the money was theirs, the expiry was in the terms\n→ Pilot team discovers expiry punished the careful saver, not the careless spender\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Programmable money makes policy precise, so it must be better money.\"\n\nSo the conditions were written to force spending quickly, and they worked. The pilot reported high spending rates and declared success. Nobody asked whether the people who saved were harmed, or whether money that expires is still money at all. The metric measured spending. The purpose was relief, and relief was the thing that expired.\n\nWhy?\n\nProgrammability is the defining feature of a central bank digital currency pilot, and its defining risk. A payment can carry conditions on where, when, and how it is spent, enforced by the system itself rather than by rules after the fact. That precision is powerful for targeted relief, and it is dangerous for the same reason: the conditions are set by the issuer, the recipient cannot negotiate them, and the system enforces them without exception. An expiry date treats saving as a failure, and it falls hardest on the people who budget most carefully, who wait for the rent date or the bill date rather than spending immediately.\n\nPrivacy is the second question, because every condition is data: where the money was spent, when, and whether the conditions were met, all visible to the issuer in a way cash never was. Holding limits and tiered privacy are the usual answers, but they are design choices, not guarantees, and they can be changed after the pilot ends. A BA writing requirements for programmable money must ask who sets the conditions, who sees the data, what happens at expiry, and whether the recipient can appeal. If those answers live only in the terms and conditions, the pilot has built control without consent.\n\nProgrammable money changes what money is, so the conditions need the same scrutiny as the payment.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nTreat every programmable condition as a policy decision with a human cost, and write the safeguards into the requirements, not the marketing.\n\n→ Conditions, who sets them, who can change them, who can appeal them\n→ Expiry, what happens to unspent value, and who bears the loss\n→ Privacy, what the issuer sees, and what tiered privacy actually protects\n→ Holding limits, how much programmable money one person may hold, and why\n→ Inclusion, whether conditions exclude the people the payment was meant to help\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Can we program the payment?\"\n\nIt is \"Whose interests do the conditions serve, and what happens to the person who cannot meet them?\"\n\nWould you accept money that expired if you did not spend it fast enough?", "id": "WC-47", "level": "advanced", "module": "Advanced", "seq": 47, "source": "Public post", "takeaway": "Programmable money changes what money is, so the conditions need the same scrutiny as the payment.", "title": "EUR 300 that expired before it could be spent", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "EUR 300 with an expiry date", "parts": [{"label": "Conditions", "note": "Who sets them, who can change them, who can appeal"}, {"label": "Expiry", "note": "Unspent value returns to the issuer on day 31"}, {"label": "Privacy", "note": "Every condition is data visible to the issuer"}, {"label": "Holding limits", "note": "How much programmable money one person may hold"}, {"label": "Inclusion", "note": "Conditions that exclude the people relief was meant for"}, {"label": "Appeal", "note": "A route to challenge a condition, not just terms to accept"}]}}, "week": 47, "agenda": "Work through this case: EUR 300 that expired before it could be spent. A relief payment of EUR 300 arrived with an expiry date. Spend it in 30 days, or lose it. The recipient saved EUR 120 toward rent, and watched it disappear. The money had done exactly what it was programmed to do. One thing to remember: Programmable money changes what money is, so the conditions need the same scrutiny as the payment.", "thumbnail": "assets/thumbs/case-WC-47.svg"}, {"explain": "A new vendor was onboarded on a Monday.\nIts first invoice was paid on the same day, EUR 18,500.\nAn AI agent had done both, the onboarding and the payment.\nThe vendor's bank details had come from a spoofed email.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ Email arrives with an invoice and new bank details for a known supplier name\n→ Agent creates the vendor record, copying the bank details from the email\n→ Agent validates the invoice against the vendor record it just created\n→ Agent approves the payment, acting as maker and checker in one step\n→ EUR 18,500 sent to an account controlled by the fraudster\n→ Real supplier chases payment, fraud discovered, funds already moved on\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"If the agent checks the invoice against the vendor record, the payment is verified.\"\n\nSo the validation passed every time, because the agent was checking its own work against its own work. The invoice matched the record, because the record had been copied from the invoice. The control was a circle, and the fraud walked straight through the middle of it. Every step in the design had been followed. The design itself was the defect.\n\nWhy?\n\nSegregation of duties exists because the person who creates a payee must never be the person who pays that payee, and the same rule applies to an agent. Onboarding a vendor and approving its first payment are two different risks: the first decides who gets paid, the second decides that money moves. When one actor does both, there is no independent check, and a single spoofed email compromises the whole chain. Bank detail changes are the highest risk moment in vendor management, because they redirect future payments, and the standard control is a callback to a known number, a number from the original contract, never from the email requesting the change.\n\nAn agent cannot make that call, and a design that lets it skip the call has removed the control rather than automated it. Maker checker is not bureaucracy. It is the principle that a second, independent actor must confirm a decision before money moves, with a limit, a named approver, and evidence of the approval. The agent was efficient, consistent, and unsupervised, and efficiency without independence is exactly how authorised push payment fraud succeeds. The question is never whether the agent followed its steps. It is whether any step was checked by someone the agent could not control.\n\nAn agent that approves its own setup has not been controlled, it has been trusted blindly.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nDraw the agent's boundary before you automate anything: what it may prepare, what it may never approve, and which human check stands between a new payee and the first payment.\n\n→ Vendor onboarding, who may create a payee, and what evidence is required\n→ Bank detail changes, callback to a known number, never to the email sender\n→ Segregation of duties, onboarding and payment approval never in the same hands\n→ Maker checker, independent approval with a limit, a named approver, and evidence\n→ Agent boundaries, what the agent may prepare, and what only a human may approve\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Can the agent onboard and pay faster?\"\n\nIt is \"What independent check stands between a new payee and the first payment to that payee?\"\n\nHave you ever seen a control that checked its own work and called it verified?", "id": "WC-48", "level": "advanced", "module": "Advanced", "seq": 48, "source": "Public post", "takeaway": "An agent that approves its own setup has not been controlled, it has been trusted blindly.", "title": "An AI agent created a vendor, then paid it EUR 18,500", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Spoofed email", "note": "Invoice and new bank details arrive for a known supplier name."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Vendor created", "note": "Agent copies bank details from the email into the record."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Invoice validated", "note": "Agent checks the invoice against the record it just made."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Independent approval?", "note": "No, the agent acts as maker and checker in one step."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Payment sent", "note": "EUR 18,500 moves to an account the fraudster controls."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Fraud discovered", "note": "Real supplier chases payment, funds already moved on."}]}}, "week": 48, "agenda": "Work through this case: An AI agent created a vendor, then paid it EUR 18,500. A new vendor was onboarded on a Monday. Its first invoice was paid on the same day, EUR 18,500. An AI agent had done both, the onboarding and the payment. The vendor's bank details had come from a spoofed email. One thing to remember: An agent that approves its own setup has not been controlled, it has been trusted blindly.", "thumbnail": "assets/thumbs/case-WC-48.svg"}, {"explain": "A EUR 250,000 payment was held two hours before the cut-off.\nThe legal entity identifier had one character transposed.\nFixing it meant missing the customer's deadline.\nSending it meant filing a report the bank knew was wrong.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ Customer instructs EUR 250,000 payment for a completion deadline that day\n→ Payment carries the beneficiary's legal entity identifier for regulatory reporting\n→ Validation fails, one character transposed, check digits do not compute\n→ Operations raise a repair, customer confirms the correct identifier by phone\n→ Repair queue is long, cut-off approaches, payment still held\n→ Choice due, repair and miss the deadline, or release with a known reporting error\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"No payment may be released with an invalid identifier, whatever the deadline.\"\n\nSo the payment waited, correctly, while the deadline approached. The customer missed the completion, and the costs were real. The rule had protected the report and harmed the customer, and nobody in the chain had the authority to weigh one against the other. The operations team followed the rule. The customer paid for a defect that was never theirs.\n\nWhy?\n\nA legal entity identifier is a 20 character code that identifies a legal entity uniquely, and its final two characters are check digits computed from the rest. One transposed character breaks the check digits, which means the code identifies nobody, or worse, a different entity. Reporting built on a wrong identifier is not a small error, it attributes a transaction to the wrong legal person, and regulators treat reporting accuracy as a duty, not a preference. A lapsed identifier, one whose annual renewal has not been completed, adds a further judgement, because it still identifies the entity but signals that its reference data may be stale.\n\nBut the identifier should have been validated long before the cut-off, at onboarding when the beneficiary was first recorded, and at instruction when the customer entered it, with a lookup that confirms the code is valid and current. The dilemma at the cut-off is manufactured by late validation: by the time the payment is held, every option is bad. The fix is not a faster repair queue. The fix is validating identifiers at the point of entry, with a repair path that carries the customer's deadline as its priority, not the queue's order.\n\nValidate identifiers at entry, because a defect found at the cut-off has no good outcome left.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nPush validation to the earliest moment the data is touched, and give the repair path a priority rule that reflects the customer's deadline, not the queue's convenience.\n\n→ Identifier validation, check digits and status checked at onboarding and at instruction\n→ Lapsed status, a signal about stale data, handled by policy, not by surprise\n→ Repair priority, customer deadlines set the queue order, not arrival time\n→ Reporting duty, accuracy is a regulatory obligation, not an internal preference\n→ Escalation authority, someone named who may weigh accuracy against deadline, with the decision recorded\n\nThe real requirement is not \"How fast can repairs clear the queue?\"\n\nIt is \"How do we catch an invalid identifier at entry, so no customer deadline ever depends on a repair?\"\n\nHave you ever watched a one character error hold up a payment that could not wait?", "id": "WC-49", "level": "advanced", "module": "Advanced", "seq": 49, "source": "Public post", "takeaway": "Validate identifiers at entry, because a defect found at the cut-off has no good outcome left.", "title": "One wrong character in an LEI held a EUR 250,000 payment", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Instruction received", "note": "EUR 250,000 payment for a completion deadline today."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Identifier valid?", "note": "One character transposed, check digits do not compute."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Repair raised", "note": "Customer confirms the correct identifier by phone."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Cut-off approaches", "note": "Repair queue is long, payment still held."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Accuracy or deadline?", "note": "Repair and miss the deadline, or release a known error."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Payment held", "note": "Rule protects the report, customer misses completion."}]}}, "week": 49, "agenda": "Work through this case: One wrong character in an LEI held a EUR 250,000 payment. A EUR 250,000 payment was held two hours before the cut-off. The legal entity identifier had one character transposed. Fixing it meant missing the customer's deadline. Sending it meant filing a report the bank knew was wrong. One thing to remember: Validate identifiers at entry, because a defect found at the cut-off has no good outcome left.", "thumbnail": "assets/thumbs/case-WC-49.svg"}, {"explain": "Two banks merged, and two payment hubs kept running.\nThe same customer name existed in both, mapped to different accounts.\nA EUR 75,000 payment followed the wrong mapping.\nThe money reached the wrong legal entity, in the wrong country.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ Merger completes, two payment hubs run in parallel during migration\n→ Customer records duplicated, same name, different entity, different account mapping\n→ Payment instructed by name, hub matches the first record it finds\n→ Funds credited to the wrong entity's account at the beneficiary bank\n→ Receiving entity queries the credit, sender discovers the error days later\n→ Recovery needs the unintended beneficiary's cooperation, return is not automatic\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Matching on customer name is good enough, because names are unique.\"\n\nSo the migration matched records by name, and names are not unique. Groups share names across entities, subsidiaries trade under the parent's name, and a single spelling difference creates a second record for the same entity. The hub did what it was told. The instruction was wrong, and the payment followed the instruction faithfully to the wrong destination.\n\nWhy?\n\nEntity identity in payments is data, not a name. A name is a label that many legal entities can share, while an account number, a bank identifier, and a legal entity identifier together pin down exactly one entity. Duplicate records are the predictable result of running two hubs in parallel without a golden record, a single mastered view of each customer and each entity, built before migration, not during it. The payment itself followed the rules of the hub it entered, and the hub's routing was only as good as its master data.\n\nOnce funds are credited to the wrong entity, the sender cannot simply pull them back: the credit is a completed payment to an account, and a return needs the beneficiary bank and the account holder to agree, which takes days and sometimes legal action. The merger dilemma is timing: consolidate quickly and break live payments, or run in parallel and let duplicates grow. The answer is not speed in either direction. It is entity resolution before cutover, matching on identifiers, not names, with every duplicate reviewed by a human who understands the group structure, and a freeze on new mappings until the golden record is agreed.\n\nA name is not an identity, and a payment routed by name alone will eventually reach the wrong entity.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nProve identity before you migrate payments. A hub consolidation is a master data programme first and a systems programme second.\n\n→ Golden record, one mastered view of each customer and entity, built before migration\n→ Entity resolution, matching on identifiers, with human review of every duplicate\n→ Parallel running, a defined end date, and a freeze on new mappings during it\n→ Routing rules, account and identifier driven, never name driven alone\n→ Recovery, a credited payment needs cooperation to return, so prevention is the control\n\nThe real requirement is not \"How fast can we consolidate the hubs?\"\n\nIt is \"How do we prove every customer record maps to exactly one legal entity before a single payment is migrated?\"\n\nHave you ever found the same customer twice in the same system, with different accounts?", "id": "WC-50", "level": "advanced", "module": "Advanced", "seq": 50, "source": "Public post", "takeaway": "A name is not an identity, and a payment routed by name alone will eventually reach the wrong entity.", "title": "After the merger, EUR 75,000 reached the wrong legal entity", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Two hubs, one name, wrong entity", "parts": [{"label": "Golden record", "note": "One mastered view of each customer and entity"}, {"label": "Entity resolution", "note": "Matching on identifiers, human review of duplicates"}, {"label": "Parallel running", "note": "A defined end date, freeze on new mappings"}, {"label": "Routing rules", "note": "Account and identifier driven, never name driven alone"}, {"label": "Duplicate records", "note": "Same name, different entity, different account mapping"}, {"label": "Recovery", "note": "A credited payment needs cooperation to return"}]}}, "week": 50, "agenda": "Work through this case: After the merger, EUR 75,000 reached the wrong legal entity. Two banks merged, and two payment hubs kept running. The same customer name existed in both, mapped to different accounts. A EUR 75,000 payment followed the wrong mapping. The money reached the wrong legal entity, in the wrong country. One thing to remember: A name is not an identity, and a payment routed by name alone will eventually reach the wrong entity.", "thumbnail": "assets/thumbs/case-WC-50.svg"}, {"explain": "A sanctions list update arrived at 13:40.\nEleven payments already in flight matched a newly listed name.\nThree had settled, five were in clearing, three were still queued.\nThe team had to decide, in the hour, which payments to stop.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ Morning, payments screened against the current list, all clear, released normally\n→ 13:40, updated list published, new name matches a beneficiary on 11 payments\n→ Three payments settled, funds already credited, recall is the only route\n→ Five payments in clearing, stop requests sent, outcome depends on timing\n→ Three payments queued, held immediately, customers told nothing yet\n→ Screening policy silent on re-screening, team improvises, decisions inconsistent\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Screening once, at instruction, is enough.\"\n\nSo the design screened at one moment and treated the result as permanent. Lists change, names are added, and a payment that was clean at 9am can be sanctioned by 2pm. The screening decision had an expiry date nobody had written down, and the first time the team discovered it was the day eleven payments were already moving.\n\nWhy?\n\nSanctions screening is a point in time decision, but sanctions liability is continuous. A bank that processes a payment for a listed person after the listing takes effect can be in breach even when the payment was screened and cleared hours earlier, because the duty is not to have screened, it is not to have facilitated the transaction. That is why the scope of re-screening must be defined by payment state before an update lands: queued payments can be held, payments in clearing can sometimes be stopped, and settled payments can only be recalled, which needs the beneficiary bank's help.\n\nEach state needs a different procedure, a different owner, and a different customer message, and the procedures must be rehearsed, because a list update does not wait for a committee. False positives complicate every decision, since a name match is not proof of identity, and holding a payment on a weak match harms an innocent customer. The screening engine must score matches, the operations team must resolve them with identifiers and dates of birth where available, and the policy must say who may release a held payment and on what evidence. The worst outcome is not a held payment. It is eleven payments handled eleven different ways, because the policy was silent.\n\nScreening is a decision with an expiry date, and the re-screening scope must be written before the list changes.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nWrite the re-screening playbook before the next list update, state by state, owner by owner, and rehearse it like the incident it is.\n\n→ Screening points, at instruction, at release, and on every list change\n→ Payment states, queued, in clearing, settled, each with its own stop procedure\n→ Match resolution, identifiers and evidence, not name alone, before a payment is held or released\n→ Ownership, a named team and a named decision maker for list updates, at any hour\n→ Customer communication, a held payment explained honestly, without disclosing the screening reason\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Did we screen the payment?\"\n\nIt is \"What happens to every payment in every state when the list changes mid-day?\"\n\nHave you ever had to stop a payment that was already on its way?", "id": "WC-51", "level": "advanced", "module": "Advanced", "seq": 51, "source": "Public post", "takeaway": "Screening is a decision with an expiry date, and the re-screening scope must be written before the list changes.", "title": "A sanctions list update landed at 13:40, with 11 payments in flight", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Payments screened", "note": "Morning screening against the current list, all clear."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "List update arrives", "note": "13:40, new name matches a beneficiary on 11 payments."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Payment state?", "note": "Queued, in clearing, or settled, each needs its own route."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Queued held", "note": "Three payments held immediately, before release."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "In clearing stopped", "note": "Five stop requests sent, outcome depends on timing."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Settled recalled", "note": "Three recalls requested, beneficiary bank help needed."}]}}, "week": 51, "agenda": "Work through this case: A sanctions list update landed at 13:40, with 11 payments in flight. A sanctions list update arrived at 13:40. Eleven payments already in flight matched a newly listed name. Three had settled, five were in clearing, three were still queued. The team had to decide, in the hour, which payments to stop. One thing to remember: Screening is a decision with an expiry date, and the re-screening scope must be written before the list changes.", "thumbnail": "assets/thumbs/case-WC-51.svg"}, {"explain": "A tester sent EUR 1 to prove the new payment chain worked.\nThe euro travelled through instruction, tracking, clearing, liquidity, exception, and return.\nIt never reached the beneficiary.\nAnd it taught the team more than a year of successful payments had.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ pain.001 raised, EUR 1, end to end reference and gpi tracking identifier attached\n→ pacs.008 sent to the beneficiary bank, pacs.002 confirms acceptance, status ACCP\n→ Tracking shows the payment accepted, settlement status ACSP\n→ Clearing system batches the payment, settlement account funded from intraday liquidity\n→ Beneficiary bank cannot apply the credit, account number fails validation\n→ Exception raised, pacs.028 and pacs.029 exchanged\n→ Return initiated, pacs.004, invalid creditor account number, funds come back\n→ Tester reconciles the return, the EUR 1 is home, the defect is found\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"A EUR 1 test is trivial, so it will pass without teaching us anything.\"\n\nSo the test was scheduled last, given no owner, and expected to be a formality. Instead it exercised every message, status, and team in the chain, and it found the one defect that mattered: account validation at the beneficiary bank rejected a valid test account, because the test data had never been loaded. A production payment would have failed the same way, for a real customer.\n\nWhy?\n\nThe EUR 1 payment is the whole journey in miniature. The instruction carries the debtor, the creditor, and the agents, and the tracking identifier follows it from bank to bank, so every status matches one payment. The pacs.002 statuses tell the story: accepted (ACCP), settlement in progress (ACSP), settlement completed (ACSC), or rejected (RJCT), and each status is evidence, not decoration. Clearing and settlement are where the money actually moves, funded by intraday liquidity that must be in the right account at the right moment.\n\nThe exception is where the lesson lives: the beneficiary bank cannot apply the credit, the investigation messages chase the facts, and the return brings the money home with a reason code that says exactly what went wrong. The return reason, invalid creditor account number, is not a failure of the test. It is the test succeeding: the return path works, the reason code is correct, and reconciliation matches the return to the original instruction. Handling the exception cost many times the value of the payment, and that ratio is the lesson: a chain that only works when nothing goes wrong does not work at all.\n\nA payment chain is only proven when its exception path works, and a EUR 1 test can prove the whole chain.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nWalk the EUR 1 through every stage before you claim the chain works, and demand evidence at each one.\n\n→ Instruction, pain.001, debtor, creditor, and agents correctly identified\n→ Tracking, the gpi identifier followed end to end, every status matched\n→ Clearing and settlement, batches, settlement accounts, intraday liquidity in place\n→ Statuses, pacs.002 evidence at each stage, accepted, in progress, completed, or rejected\n→ Exception, investigation messages, facts chased, not assumed\n→ Return, pacs.004 with a correct reason code, reconciled to the original instruction\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Did the EUR 1 arrive?\"\n\nIt is \"Can we prove, with message evidence, what happened to the payment at every stage, including when it came back?\"\n\nHave you ever learned more from a payment that failed than from a hundred that succeeded?", "id": "WC-52", "level": "advanced", "module": "Advanced", "seq": 52, "source": "Public post", "takeaway": "A payment chain is only proven when its exception path works, and a EUR 1 test can prove the whole chain.", "title": "The EUR 1 test payment that taught the whole journey", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Instruction", "note": "pain.001 raised, EUR 1, gpi tracking identifier attached."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Tracking", "note": "pacs.008 sent, pacs.002 confirms acceptance, ACCP."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Clearing", "note": "Payment batched, settlement status ACSP."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Settlement", "note": "Settlement account funded from intraday liquidity."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Credit applied?", "note": "Account number fails validation, credit cannot be applied."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Investigation", "note": "pacs.028 and pacs.029 chase the facts."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Return", "note": "pacs.004 returns the EUR 1 with the correct reason code."}]}}, "week": 52, "agenda": "Work through this case: The EUR 1 test payment that taught the whole journey. A tester sent EUR 1 to prove the new payment chain worked. The euro travelled through instruction, tracking, clearing, liquidity, exception, and return. It never reached the beneficiary. And it taught the team more than a year of successful payments had. One thing to remember: A payment chain is only proven when its exception path works, and a EUR 1 test can prove the whole chain.", "thumbnail": "assets/thumbs/case-WC-52.svg"}];
